WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:03.895 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.895 --> 00:00:04.865 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.865 --> 00:00:09.020 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.520 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So then
let's get started

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.920 align:middle line:84%
with our second-to-last
topic today, which

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.420 align:middle line:84%
is going to be something
called cheap talk.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.040 align:middle line:90%


00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.460 align:middle line:84%
I think it's good to have
some context for this.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.280 align:middle line:84%
So far, we've looked
at signaling games.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.439 align:middle line:84%
And so let's recall that when
we looked at signaling games,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.560 align:middle line:84%
people with private information
were conveying information

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.820 align:middle line:84%
to some other party
through costly actions.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:54.280 align:middle line:84%
So under signaling, info
is conveyed through costly

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.860 align:middle line:84%
or we might also say
payoff-relevant actions.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:03.360 align:middle line:90%


00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.880 align:middle line:84%
So one way to convey information
about how smart you are

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.222 align:middle line:84%
or how good of an employee
you are is to go to college.

00:01:08.222 --> 00:01:10.680 align:middle line:84%
And the whole point of that
model was that going to college

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.560 align:middle line:84%
is costly and
differentially costly

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.860 align:middle line:90%
for different kinds of people.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.560 align:middle line:84%
Or when a firm was
conveying information

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.520 align:middle line:84%
about the quality of an item,
they were setting a price.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.420 align:middle line:84%
And maybe a price
isn't directly costly,

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.600 align:middle line:84%
but it is payoff relevant,
because the price I set

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.520 align:middle line:84%
affects how much money I get if
you actually accept my offer.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.320 align:middle line:84%
But in the real world, we
see a lot of information

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.702 align:middle line:84%
conveyed not through
costly actions,

00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:35.660 align:middle line:84%
but just through speech,
through communication.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:36.785 align:middle line:90%
We send messages to people.

00:01:36.785 --> 00:01:37.460 align:middle line:90%
We use words.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.760 align:middle line:90%
We talk to people.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.100 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, when
there's a conflict of interest,

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.860 align:middle line:84%
can we also convey information
through costless actions?

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.520 align:middle line:84%
So today, the
difference is we're

00:01:47.520 --> 00:02:00.350 align:middle line:84%
going to look at conveying
information through costless--

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.350 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes, instead
of calling these actions,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.310 align:middle line:90%
you might call them messages.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.602 align:middle line:84%
Because we usually think of
speech or words or messages

00:02:05.602 --> 00:02:06.350 align:middle line:90%
as being costly.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.250 align:middle line:90%


00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.850 align:middle line:84%
Now, there are
certainly some contexts

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.142 align:middle line:90%
where this isn't going to work.

00:02:13.142 --> 00:02:16.130 align:middle line:84%
If we think of the job
market signaling example,

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.680 align:middle line:84%
the firm could ask you how
good of an employee are you.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, everyone's going to
say they're a good employee.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.610 align:middle line:84%
So there are some contexts where
we really need a costly action

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.030 align:middle line:90%
in order to make it credible.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.890 align:middle line:84%
The information that
we're going to convey.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.810 align:middle line:84%
There are other situations
where it's clear

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.550 align:middle line:90%
that communication is credible.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.217 align:middle line:84%
If our interests are
completely aligned.

00:02:32.217 --> 00:02:34.383 align:middle line:84%
Then of course, we can
communicate freely and that's

00:02:34.383 --> 00:02:36.810 align:middle line:84%
not going to be difficult. So
the point of the model today

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.610 align:middle line:84%
is going to show that
sometimes, there will

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.110 align:middle line:90%
be a misalignment of interests.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.693 align:middle line:84%
There will be a
conflict of interests.

00:02:41.693 --> 00:02:44.090 align:middle line:84%
Yet nevertheless, some
kind of communication

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.410 align:middle line:84%
can be credible, in
contrast to the model

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.830 align:middle line:84%
of telling the employer
that you're a good employee.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.372 align:middle line:84%
So that's going to be the model
we're going to look at today.

00:02:51.372 --> 00:02:55.250 align:middle line:90%


00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:00.050 align:middle line:84%
So this model is based on a
paper by Crawford and Sobel

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:01.810 align:middle line:90%
in 1982.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:08.050 align:middle line:84%
So we're getting closer
to modern topics.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.970 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to think of--
this is a game between a sender.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.810 align:middle line:84%
We call them sender
and receiver.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.830 align:middle line:90%
But these are abstract labels.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.330 align:middle line:84%
I want you to
think of the sender

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.730 align:middle line:84%
as an advisor and the
receiver as a decision maker.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.930 align:middle line:84%
And the presidential advisor
might collect information

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.730 align:middle line:84%
and make a recommendation
to the president about what

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.690 align:middle line:84%
tax policy should be, or to
the Fed about what the interest

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:35.070 align:middle line:84%
rate should be, or, how much we
should spend on the military.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.590 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is that
the sender, the advisor,

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.723 align:middle line:84%
has more information
than the decision maker,

00:03:39.723 --> 00:03:41.390 align:middle line:84%
because the decision
maker is very busy.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.030 align:middle line:84%
The CEO can't know
about everything.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:46.890 align:middle line:84%
They talk to a consultant
or a researcher

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.270 align:middle line:84%
who's going to be very informed
about this particular issue.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.270 align:middle line:90%
So how do we model this?

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:59.570 align:middle line:84%
Well, the sender is going to
privately observe what We're

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.410 align:middle line:90%
going to call maybe a state.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:07.010 align:middle line:84%
Or this could also be
called their type t in 0, 1.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.170 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to think of
this in our economic example.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.907 align:middle line:90%
How good is the economy?

00:04:11.907 --> 00:04:13.490 align:middle line:84%
What's the ideal
amount of spending we

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:14.747 align:middle line:90%
should spend on the military?

00:04:14.747 --> 00:04:15.830 align:middle line:90%
What's the ideal tax rate?

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.330 align:middle line:84%
So this makes sense
for any decision

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:20.769 align:middle line:84%
where there's this
one-dimensional choice

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:22.770 align:middle line:90%
that we have to make.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.490 align:middle line:84%
We can think of this usually
as the state of the world.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.470 align:middle line:84%
But we'll also call
it the sender's type,

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.370 align:middle line:84%
because this is private
information that the sender has.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.890 align:middle line:84%
And then the sender, after
observing their type t,

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:37.850 align:middle line:90%
is going to send a message m--

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.250 align:middle line:84%
we'll just call it, in
some abstract set, big M--

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.170 align:middle line:90%
to the receiver.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:48.670 align:middle line:84%
And then the receiver is
going to observe M but not t.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.220 align:middle line:84%
The whole point is if the
receiver, the decision maker,

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.660 align:middle line:84%
understood the
state of the world,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.120 align:middle line:84%
knew what the
right decision was,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.380 align:middle line:84%
they wouldn't need help
from the advisor anyway.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.140 align:middle line:84%
So they don't
directly observe t,

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.500 align:middle line:84%
but they do observe this message
that's sent by the sender.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.820 align:middle line:84%
And then after that, they choose
an action or decision which

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:08.980 align:middle line:90%
we'll call y.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.540 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to
be a real number.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.002 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll say--

00:05:14.002 --> 00:05:15.960 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if I've used
this notation before.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.560 align:middle line:84%
This is just the
set of real numbers.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.780 align:middle line:90%


00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, whenever we
talk about something

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.300 align:middle line:84%
being privately observed, we
need to specify the distribution

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.290 align:middle line:90%
from which this comes.

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:28.540 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to assume
that the state, just to make

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.460 align:middle line:90%
things easy, is uniform.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.880 align:middle line:84%
So just to be clear, the
sender knows what the state is.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:35.980 align:middle line:90%
They know their type.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.120 align:middle line:84%
The uniform distribution is
relevant to the receiver.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.840 align:middle line:84%
The receiver believes that this
state is uniformly distributed,

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.100 align:middle line:84%
but they don't know what the
actual value of the state is.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.560 align:middle line:90%
I'll say uniformly distributed.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.340 align:middle line:90%


00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.892 align:middle line:90%
And now let me specify payoffs.

00:05:52.892 --> 00:05:54.600 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this is a
very stylized model,

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.300 align:middle line:84%
but I think it captures a
lot of policy disagreements

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.380 align:middle line:90%
that we have.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:03.460 align:middle line:84%
So the sender, their payoff
u s depends on the decision y

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:08.220 align:middle line:84%
that the receiver takes and
also t, the state of the world.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.440 align:middle line:90%
And same for the receiver.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.460 align:middle line:90%


00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.100 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to
imagine for the receiver,

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.060 align:middle line:84%
to make things simple,
is the receiver just

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.540 align:middle line:84%
wants to match their decision
with the state of the world.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:25.100 align:middle line:84%
So we could think of
the state of the world

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:30.700 align:middle line:84%
might be some measure of
how close the economy is

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.140 align:middle line:90%
to a recession.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:37.260 align:middle line:84%
And y might be how much we're
planning to cut interest rates.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.260 align:middle line:84%
Or t might be the
severity or the risk

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.860 align:middle line:84%
of some military conflict, and
y might be the spending level

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.320 align:middle line:90%
that we spend on that conflict.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.520 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is, these
things move together.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.660 align:middle line:84%
The higher the state, the
higher the ideal decision

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:50.540 align:middle line:90%
for the receiver.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:55.780 align:middle line:84%
So we'll formally model this
with the quadratic loss function

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.300 align:middle line:90%
that we said before.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.540 align:middle line:84%
So this means what the
receiver would love to do

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:01.695 align:middle line:84%
is have the decision
exactly match the state.

00:07:01.695 --> 00:07:03.820 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of the
state as basically measuring

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.180 align:middle line:84%
what would be the ideal
decision for the receiver

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.965 align:middle line:90%
if they could observe the state.

00:07:07.965 --> 00:07:09.340 align:middle line:84%
And then the
receiver experiences

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.900 align:middle line:84%
a loss that's equal
to the difference

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.243 align:middle line:84%
between their decision
and the state squared.

00:07:14.243 --> 00:07:16.160 align:middle line:84%
So how far off the
decision is from the state.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:16.700 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So
basically, the decision

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.420 align:middle line:84%
is just a reflection of
what the receiver believes

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:21.600 align:middle line:90%
that the state is?

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.740 align:middle line:90%


00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.660 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It's true that their
preferences over the decision

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.565 align:middle line:84%
depend on their beliefs
about the state.

00:07:29.565 --> 00:07:31.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But [INAUDIBLE]
exactly match the state, right?

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.620 align:middle line:90%
So it should be--

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:37.100 align:middle line:84%
so is the decision
supposed to be a state?

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.380 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: No, I wouldn't.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:42.240 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe one way of
interpreting this is, OK,

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.140 align:middle line:84%
if we want to think
of a richer model,

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.180 align:middle line:84%
probably the state
is really a very rich

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:48.740 align:middle line:90%
multidimensional object.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.700 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to denote the
state-- the aspect of the state

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:54.630 align:middle line:84%
that matters is what the ideal
decision is in that state.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.380 align:middle line:84%
So I think maybe the
better interpretation

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:58.300 align:middle line:84%
is, when we write
t, what we really

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:02.100 align:middle line:84%
mean is, what is the
receiver's ideal decision given

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.160 align:middle line:90%
the state of the world?

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:05.680 align:middle line:84%
So maybe the economy
has many dimensions.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.460 align:middle line:84%
But think of t saying the ideal
interest rate for the Fed to set

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.420 align:middle line:84%
is 3% and y being the interest
rate the Fed actually sets.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.380 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:15.660 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:16.740 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK, great.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.040 align:middle line:90%
So now what about the sender?

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.280 align:middle line:84%
Well, if the sender and receiver
have the same preferences,

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.440 align:middle line:84%
this would be a
really easy problem.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.732 align:middle line:84%
Of course the advisor is
going to just reveal the state

00:08:23.732 --> 00:08:25.780 align:middle line:84%
and allow the receiver
to make a good decision.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:27.700 align:middle line:84%
But often, advisors
and decision makers

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.040 align:middle line:90%
have misaligned interests.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.457 align:middle line:84%
And the way we're
going to capture

00:08:30.457 --> 00:08:36.306 align:middle line:84%
that here is we're going to
say the sender's preferences

00:08:36.306 --> 00:08:39.020 align:middle line:90%
is like this, where beta--

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:40.159 align:middle line:90%
I'll put it down here--

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.990 align:middle line:90%


00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.630 align:middle line:90%
is greater than 0.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.970 align:middle line:84%
So here, beta is
called the bias.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:51.230 align:middle line:90%


00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.230 align:middle line:84%
This is the bias that
the sender or the advisor

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:55.350 align:middle line:84%
has relative to
the decision maker.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:58.310 align:middle line:84%
So notice that, well,
the receiver always

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.230 align:middle line:84%
wants to match the
decision with the state.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:04.670 align:middle line:84%
The sender would like to match
the decision not with the state,

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:07.750 align:middle line:84%
but with the state
plus this bias beta.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.670 align:middle line:84%
So notice some characteristics
of this misalignment

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:12.150 align:middle line:90%
of interest.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.350 align:middle line:84%
Both the sender and
the receiver agree

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:19.070 align:middle line:84%
that when the state is higher,
a higher decision is warranted.

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:22.590 align:middle line:84%
What they disagree about is the
exact value of the decision.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:24.950 align:middle line:84%
So whatever the state
is, the sender always

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.288 align:middle line:84%
thinks the decision should
be a little bit higher

00:09:27.288 --> 00:09:28.330 align:middle line:90%
than the receiver thinks.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.027 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is consistent
with a lot of policy

00:09:31.027 --> 00:09:31.610 align:middle line:90%
disagreements.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.970 align:middle line:84%
Let's take the
example of the Fed.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.345 align:middle line:84%
There are some people who are
more hawkish or more dovish

00:09:35.345 --> 00:09:35.887 align:middle line:90%
on inflation.

00:09:35.887 --> 00:09:38.178 align:middle line:84%
So some people always tend
to want lower interest rates

00:09:38.178 --> 00:09:38.970 align:middle line:90%
than someone else.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.370 align:middle line:84%
But everyone agrees that when
the economy is in worse shape,

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:42.950 align:middle line:84%
we should tend to
cut interest rates,

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:44.410 align:middle line:84%
and when the economy
is doing well,

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:45.952 align:middle line:84%
maybe we should
raise interest rates.

00:09:45.952 --> 00:09:48.910 align:middle line:84%
So they all agree about the
directional effect of the state

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:50.170 align:middle line:90%
on the ideal decision.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:53.210 align:middle line:84%
They just disagree slightly
about what the level is.

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:55.550 align:middle line:84%
And the sender always
wants a decision

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:58.710 align:middle line:84%
that's higher than what
the receiver wants exactly

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:00.790 align:middle line:90%
by this amount beta.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.870 align:middle line:84%
So the larger beta
is, the greater

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:05.890 align:middle line:84%
the disagreement between
the sender and the receiver.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:06.890 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here's our model.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.930 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about
the model so far?

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.270 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:14.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does beta just
depend on who the sender is?

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:17.168 align:middle line:84%
It's not like distributed,
it's just like [INAUDIBLE]?

00:10:17.168 --> 00:10:18.210 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Good question.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.030 align:middle line:84%
So you could imagine
a richer model

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:23.150 align:middle line:84%
where the decision maker doesn't
know the political biases

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.210 align:middle line:90%
of the advisor.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.430 align:middle line:84%
And that's actually an
interesting extension

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:26.410 align:middle line:90%
that people have looked at.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:28.910 align:middle line:84%
But for today, we're going to
assume beta is commonly known.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.210 align:middle line:90%
So I hire this advisor.

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:33.310 align:middle line:84%
I know that my
advisor always tends

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:34.650 align:middle line:90%
to have this policy preference.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:35.170 align:middle line:90%
And this is known.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:36.930 align:middle line:84%
What I don't know is the
true state of the world.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:37.450 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:38.330 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:40.950 align:middle line:90%


00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.370 align:middle line:90%
OK, so it's a game.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:45.130 align:middle line:84%
Let's first think about what
strategies are in this game.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:51.390 align:middle line:90%


00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:55.010 align:middle line:84%
So the sender, they observe
the state of the world

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:56.790 align:middle line:90%
or they observe their type t.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.870 align:middle line:84%
And they choose what message
to send to the receiver.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:09.430 align:middle line:84%
So a strategy for the sender
is a function m from 0, 1

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:15.030 align:middle line:84%
to this abstract set of messages
M. Maybe we'll go into here just

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:17.190 align:middle line:90%
to understand what goes on.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:20.950 align:middle line:84%
So what this says is if I see
that the state is, say, 0.3,

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:23.150 align:middle line:84%
this is the message
I send, m of 0.3.

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:25.150 align:middle line:84%
If I see that the
state is 0.7, then I

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:28.790 align:middle line:84%
might send a different
message, m of 0.7.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:31.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, the receiver's strategy,
well, what do they do?

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:33.050 align:middle line:90%
They see the message.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.870 align:middle line:84%
And based on the
message that they see,

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.750 align:middle line:90%
they choose a decision to make.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:41.560 align:middle line:84%
So for the receiver, a
strategy is a function y from M

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:48.480 align:middle line:84%
to we'll say R. Just to
understand what's going on,

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.480 align:middle line:90%
I'll write it like this.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.200 align:middle line:84%
So the receiver says, if I get
a message m from my advisor,

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.540 align:middle line:84%
this is the decision I'm
going to take, y of m.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:02.920 align:middle line:90%


00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:08.000 align:middle line:84%
And the solution concept
we're going to work with

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:09.700 align:middle line:90%
is going to be PBE, as before--

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:11.900 align:middle line:90%
Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium.

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:14.920 align:middle line:90%


00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:18.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's start
by analyzing how

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:21.400 align:middle line:90%
things work for the receiver.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So with perfect Bayesian
equilibrium, whatever

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.880 align:middle line:84%
message the receiver
sees, they're

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.480 align:middle line:84%
going to update their belief
and then behave optimally

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.760 align:middle line:90%
given their updated belief.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:41.520 align:middle line:84%
So let's just
remember that whenever

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.540 align:middle line:84%
we work with perfect
Bayesian equilibrium,

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:48.000 align:middle line:84%
we have to distinguish between
the messages that occur on path

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.440 align:middle line:84%
and the messages that
don't occur on path.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:55.080 align:middle line:84%
So a message m is on path if,
in a given strategy profile,

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.880 align:middle line:84%
there's some state of the
world where the sender actually

00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:59.360 align:middle line:90%
sends that message.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:02.080 align:middle line:84%
And a message is off path
if that message is not

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:05.240 align:middle line:84%
supposed to be sent in
any state of the world.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:08.280 align:middle line:84%
So if the receiver sees
an on-path message m,

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.540 align:middle line:90%
they apply Bayes' rule.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:15.240 align:middle line:90%


00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:18.920 align:middle line:84%
And then using Bayes' rule,
they have an updated belief

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.000 align:middle line:90%
about the state of the world t.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.340 align:middle line:84%
But because of the nature
of our preference relation,

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.520 align:middle line:84%
the nature of our
utility function,

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.160 align:middle line:84%
the receiver always
wants to take

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.720 align:middle line:84%
the decision that's
equal to their updated

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:31.600 align:middle line:90%
expectation of the state.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.760 align:middle line:90%
So they apply Bayes' rule.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:40.267 align:middle line:84%
They can now compute maybe
I'll say E of t given m.

00:13:40.267 --> 00:13:41.100 align:middle line:90%
What does this mean?

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:43.000 align:middle line:90%
It says, I observe m.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.600 align:middle line:84%
I update my beliefs about
the state of the world.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.273 align:middle line:84%
And I'm now going
to compute what

00:13:47.273 --> 00:13:49.440 align:middle line:84%
I think the expected value
of the state of the world

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.320 align:middle line:90%
is given my updated beliefs.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.560 align:middle line:84%
And it's always going
to be optimal for them

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.040 align:middle line:90%
to take this as their decision.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.040 align:middle line:84%
Compute that, and
in fact, that's

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:00.520 align:middle line:90%
going to be their decision.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.770 align:middle line:84%
They're going to update their
beliefs about the state,

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:06.200 align:middle line:84%
compute an expectation and set
their decision equal to that.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:13.960 align:middle line:84%
Off pat, well, the
beliefs over 0, 1

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:20.280 align:middle line:84%
are unconstrained because
we can't apply Bayes' rule.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:24.200 align:middle line:84%
So off path, the receiver
can believe anything

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.520 align:middle line:90%
about the state between 0 and 1.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.720 align:middle line:84%
So the key question
is, what decisions

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:31.970 align:middle line:84%
could the receiver take if
they see an off-path message m?

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.170 align:middle line:84%
What are the range
of decisions that are

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:35.750 align:middle line:90%
consistent with some belief?

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:40.290 align:middle line:90%


00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:40.790 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:42.210 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Just the real numbers.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:44.830 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So we might think
it's all the real numbers.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.038 align:middle line:84%
But is there any belief
that's consistent with taking

00:14:47.038 --> 00:14:48.070 align:middle line:90%
decision y equals 2?

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:50.690 align:middle line:90%


00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:53.570 align:middle line:90%
We know t lives in 0, 1.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:56.850 align:middle line:84%
So the point is-- and this
is kind of a subtle point--

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:00.630 align:middle line:84%
the point is, I can form any
belief I want about the state.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:02.757 align:middle line:84%
But we know the state
lies between 0 and 1.

00:15:02.757 --> 00:15:05.090 align:middle line:84%
So the most pessimistic belief
I could have is the state

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:06.530 align:middle line:90%
is definitely 0.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.495 align:middle line:84%
And the most
optimistic-- or here

00:15:08.495 --> 00:15:09.870 align:middle line:84%
it's not clear
what's optimistic,

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.970 align:middle line:84%
but the highest belief I
could have is the state

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:13.010 align:middle line:90%
is certainly 1.

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.130 align:middle line:84%
And in those two cases I
would take the decision

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:17.190 align:middle line:90%
y equals 0 and y equals 1.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:26.450 align:middle line:84%
So I'm always going
to take some decision.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:29.190 align:middle line:84%
y that's always going
to be between 0 and 1,

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:30.690 align:middle line:84%
because those are
the decisions that

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:33.170 align:middle line:84%
are consistent with some
belief about the state.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:35.850 align:middle line:84%
Any decision in here
can be justified

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:37.142 align:middle line:90%
by some belief about the state.

00:15:37.142 --> 00:15:39.392 align:middle line:84%
I could take decision 1/2
because I think the state is

00:15:39.392 --> 00:15:40.230 align:middle line:90%
certain to be 1/2.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:42.022 align:middle line:90%
Or maybe I think it's uniform.

00:15:42.022 --> 00:15:43.230 align:middle line:90%
Those are all going to be OK.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:47.930 align:middle line:90%


00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.090 align:middle line:84%
It's going to turn
out, as we'll see,

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.977 align:middle line:84%
that the off-path
beliefs are not

00:15:52.977 --> 00:15:54.810 align:middle line:84%
going to play as big
of a role in this model

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:56.898 align:middle line:84%
as they did in the
signaling model.

00:15:56.898 --> 00:15:58.190 align:middle line:90%
But we'll see that a bit later.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:01.130 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with,
just to build intuition,

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:05.268 align:middle line:90%
a really simple equilibrium.

00:16:05.268 --> 00:16:06.810 align:middle line:84%
And the simplest
equilibrium would be

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:08.510 align:middle line:90%
a no-communication equilibrium.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:17.935 align:middle line:90%


00:16:17.935 --> 00:16:19.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, when I say no
communication, what I mean

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:21.435 align:middle line:84%
is no information is
actually conveyed.

00:16:21.435 --> 00:16:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Of course, they're
actually going

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:24.690 align:middle line:84%
to send a message
because they have to.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.695 align:middle line:84%
But one thing the
advisor could do

00:16:26.695 --> 00:16:28.070 align:middle line:84%
is they could be
a broken record.

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:29.890 align:middle line:84%
They could just always
send the same message.

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:31.430 align:middle line:84%
And if they always
send the same message,

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:32.790 align:middle line:84%
no matter the
state of the world,

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:35.498 align:middle line:84%
then that message doesn't convey
any information about the state.

00:16:35.498 --> 00:16:37.630 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at this kind
of boring equilibrium.

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:44.080 align:middle line:84%
Well, what is-- let's fix some
message I'll call m* in m.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:46.330 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be the
message that the sender always

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.930 align:middle line:84%
sends, no matter the
state of the world.

00:16:48.930 --> 00:17:00.770 align:middle line:84%
So the sender's strategy is m
of t equals m* for all t in 01.

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:02.870 align:middle line:84%
So going to our
economic example,

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:05.470 align:middle line:84%
if they see the economy's great,
they say cut interest rates.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:07.970 align:middle line:84%
If they see the economy is bad,
they say cut interest rates.

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:09.770 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, what
they say conveys

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:13.450 align:middle line:90%
no information about the state.

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:15.490 align:middle line:84%
Now let's try to compute
what the receiver is

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:17.569 align:middle line:90%
going to do in this case.

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:22.690 align:middle line:84%
So let's now look
at the receiver.

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:29.300 align:middle line:84%
And what happens if the
receiver sees the message m*?

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:32.260 align:middle line:84%
What beliefs does the receiver
form about the state if they get

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:33.220 align:middle line:90%
this message m*?

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:37.568 align:middle line:90%


00:17:37.568 --> 00:17:38.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:39.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Were they
just going to be

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.668 align:middle line:84%
left with the prior, which is
that t is distributed over 0, 1?

00:17:42.668 --> 00:17:43.460 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:44.380 align:middle line:84%
They'd just be left
with the prior,

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:45.740 align:middle line:84%
because they know whatever
the state is, they're

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:46.640 align:middle line:90%
going to get this message.

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:47.880 align:middle line:90%
This hasn't told them anything.

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:48.860 align:middle line:84%
If you always say
the same thing,

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:50.480 align:middle line:84%
you haven't revealed
anything to me.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:53.140 align:middle line:84%
So my beliefs are the
same as the prior.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:59.200 align:middle line:84%
So if I see m*, maybe we could
say retains prior beliefs.

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:02.100 align:middle line:90%


00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.020 align:middle line:84%
That is, I still think
the state is uniformly

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:05.860 align:middle line:90%
distributed over 0, 1.

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:09.060 align:middle line:84%
And the average state
is therefore 1/2.

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:12.000 align:middle line:84%
So the best decision for
me is to take y equals 1/2.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.400 align:middle line:84%
So it retains prior
beliefs and they take--

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:20.060 align:middle line:90%
so y of m* equals 1/2.

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:22.140 align:middle line:84%
So this looks good
for equilibrium.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.680 align:middle line:90%
But we still have to worry.

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:27.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, we have to say, what does
the receiver do if they see

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.182 align:middle line:84%
a message m That's
not equal to m*?

00:18:31.182 --> 00:18:33.140 align:middle line:84%
They don't really know
what to do in this case,

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:36.042 align:middle line:84%
because they were expecting
to always see the message m*.

00:18:36.042 --> 00:18:37.500 align:middle line:84%
They can't really
apply Bayes' rule

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.980 align:middle line:84%
here because they'd
be dividing by 0.

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:41.840 align:middle line:90%
So there's a lot of flexibility.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.740 align:middle line:90%


00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:48.220 align:middle line:84%
You might say, oh, well, let's
just choose y arbitrarily.

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:54.780 align:middle line:84%
But what could go wrong if we
just chose y arbitrarily here

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:55.860 align:middle line:90%
for the receiver?

00:18:55.860 --> 00:19:03.838 align:middle line:84%
Let's say we chose y
equals 1/3 or something.

00:19:03.838 --> 00:19:04.880 align:middle line:90%
What could go wrong here?

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:05.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.005 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe the
sender will-- like,

00:19:07.005 --> 00:19:08.660 align:middle line:84%
that would be a
profitable deviation.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:09.360 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly right.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.360 align:middle line:84%
So whenever we're choosing
the off-path behavior

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:13.620 align:middle line:84%
by the receiver, we
have to keep in mind

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:15.620 align:middle line:84%
that we don't want
to choose behavior

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:18.527 align:middle line:84%
that will introduce a profitable
deviation for the sender.

00:19:18.527 --> 00:19:20.860 align:middle line:84%
In this equilibrium, the
sender is only supposed to send

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:22.140 align:middle line:90%
the message m*.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:25.180 align:middle line:84%
So we want to make sure that the
sender never wants to deviate

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:27.500 align:middle line:84%
to send any message
other than m*.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:28.520 align:middle line:90%
So how can we be sure?

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:31.260 align:middle line:84%
How could we choose y of m to
be sure that the sender never

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:32.040 align:middle line:90%
wants to deviate?

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:32.660 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:35.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You could make it so
that the difference between--

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:39.318 align:middle line:84%
you get the same utility
from getting m* and r.

00:19:39.318 --> 00:19:40.360 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly right.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.220 align:middle line:84%
And how could we make sure
they get the same utility?

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:45.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't
know exactly, but it's

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:46.785 align:middle line:90%
depending on beta, right?

00:19:46.785 --> 00:19:48.660 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I think you're
on the right track.

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:49.560 align:middle line:90%
There's even a simpler answer.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:50.200 align:middle line:90%
So you're exactly right.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:52.040 align:middle line:84%
We want to make sure that
if they deviate to this,

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:53.800 align:middle line:84%
the sender gets exactly
the same utility.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:54.675 align:middle line:90%
But what could it be?

00:19:54.675 --> 00:19:55.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:56.240 align:middle line:90%
It could just be 1/2.

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:57.720 align:middle line:84%
So what they could do
is they could say, look,

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.540 align:middle line:84%
if I see any other
message, I'm going

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:00.940 align:middle line:90%
to do exactly the same thing.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:03.160 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to
choose ym equals 1/2.

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:04.805 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to maintain
my prior belief.

00:20:04.805 --> 00:20:06.180 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand
the structure

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:08.400 align:middle line:84%
of this no-communication
equilibrium.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.820 align:middle line:84%
The receiver says, whatever
message you send me

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:12.120 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to do the same thing.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:14.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to
completely ignore you.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:15.740 align:middle line:84%
And the sender says,
well, if you're

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.200 align:middle line:84%
going to ignore what I say,
it doesn't matter what I say.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:20.950 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well always
send the message m*.

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.310 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of sometimes
goes over the name-- this

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.830 align:middle line:90%
is sometimes called babbling.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:27.910 align:middle line:84%
So in this equilibrium,
the receiver

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:30.090 align:middle line:84%
is treating his advisor's
words as just babbles.

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:30.930 align:middle line:90%
I don't care what you say.

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:32.330 align:middle line:84%
It's not going to
change my belief.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.770 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to ignore what
you say and do the same thing.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:36.410 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
advisor says, well,

00:20:36.410 --> 00:20:39.750 align:middle line:84%
I might as well always
send this message m*.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.277 align:middle line:90%
So indeed, this satisfies--

00:20:42.277 --> 00:20:44.110 align:middle line:84%
let's just check what
we would have to check

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:45.610 align:middle line:90%
to make sure this equilibrium.

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:53.990 align:middle line:84%
We'd have to check sender
optimality, receiver optimality,

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:55.450 align:middle line:90%
and belief consistency.

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:59.722 align:middle line:90%


00:20:59.722 --> 00:21:00.930 align:middle line:90%
I've been a little imprecise.

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:03.370 align:middle line:84%
I haven't really written
what the beliefs are here.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:05.770 align:middle line:84%
But naturally, the
beliefs could be,

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:07.770 align:middle line:84%
I just think the state
is uniformly distributed

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:09.270 align:middle line:90%
if I see any other message.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.830 align:middle line:84%
And then we can see that each
of these three conditions

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:14.978 align:middle line:90%
is going to be satisfied.

00:21:14.978 --> 00:21:16.770 align:middle line:84%
This is not a very
interesting equilibrium,

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:18.437 align:middle line:84%
but it's just good
to see there's always

00:21:18.437 --> 00:21:21.190 align:middle line:84%
an equilibrium like this,
where our words just don't have

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:24.550 align:middle line:90%
any meaning And no one listens.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:25.957 align:middle line:90%
Well, that's one extreme.

00:21:25.957 --> 00:21:27.290 align:middle line:90%
Let's see if we could do better.

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:30.950 align:middle line:84%
We might want to have
perfect communication.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:32.430 align:middle line:84%
So what about an
equilibrium where

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:33.810 align:middle line:90%
there's perfect communication?

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:37.245 align:middle line:90%


00:21:37.245 --> 00:21:39.870 align:middle line:84%
So you might think, well, if the
advisor and the decision maker

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:41.070 align:middle line:84%
have a pretty good
relationship, what

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:42.737 align:middle line:84%
should happen is the
advisor should just

00:21:42.737 --> 00:21:44.750 align:middle line:90%
say this is what the state is.

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:49.990 align:middle line:84%
So could there be an equilibrium
where the message m of t

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:51.150 align:middle line:90%
just equals t?

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:54.950 align:middle line:84%
So the advisor says, this is
the state, make a decision.

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:55.870 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts?

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:59.190 align:middle line:84%
Could we make this
an equilibrium?

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:00.003 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, maybe?

00:22:00.003 --> 00:22:01.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It would
not be an equilibrium

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:06.810 align:middle line:84%
because the receiver would just
act with y equals t as a result.

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:10.550 align:middle line:84%
And then the sender is losing
some amount because y does not

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:12.162 align:middle line:90%
equal t plus beta.

00:22:12.162 --> 00:22:12.870 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Right.

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:13.510 align:middle line:90%
Let's work through it.

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:15.302 align:middle line:84%
If we tried to-- this
is not going to work,

00:22:15.302 --> 00:22:16.290 align:middle line:90%
but let's go ahead.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:17.957 align:middle line:84%
If we tried to have
an equilibrium where

00:22:17.957 --> 00:22:20.030 align:middle line:84%
this was the messaging
strategy of the sender,

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:21.790 align:middle line:84%
well, this looks great
for the receiver.

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:25.010 align:middle line:84%
What is the receiver
y of t going to be?

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:26.270 align:middle line:90%
Let's be clear here.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:28.930 align:middle line:84%
The receiver's strategy is
not a function of the state.

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:30.252 align:middle line:90%
It's a function of the message.

00:22:30.252 --> 00:22:32.210 align:middle line:84%
But here, the message is
the same as the state.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.230 align:middle line:90%
So that's why I'm writing t.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:37.070 align:middle line:84%
Or actually, maybe
I'll still write m.

00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:38.650 align:middle line:84%
Eh, torn about which
notation to use.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:40.110 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't make a difference.

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.030 align:middle line:90%
I'll write m.

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:43.750 align:middle line:84%
In this case, when
they see a message m,

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.990 align:middle line:84%
they're going to say, well,
that means the state t equals m.

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:51.950 align:middle line:84%
So their best decision is
just going to be m, right?

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:55.990 align:middle line:84%
But now let's put yourself
in the shoes of the sender.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:58.530 align:middle line:84%
And let's say you see
that the state is t.

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:03.010 align:middle line:90%


00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:04.510 align:middle line:84%
If you were the
sender, what message

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:06.590 align:middle line:84%
would you send to
the decision maker?

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:10.952 align:middle line:90%


00:23:10.952 --> 00:23:13.030 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: t plus beta.

00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:14.430 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:16.872 align:middle line:90%
So you know the state is t.

00:23:16.872 --> 00:23:18.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, why don't
you just say, look,

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:21.850 align:middle line:84%
you're going to tell your
advisor m equals t plus beta?

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:25.590 align:middle line:90%


00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:28.850 align:middle line:84%
And I think we see
this all the time.

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:30.697 align:middle line:84%
If someone's more of
an interventionist

00:23:30.697 --> 00:23:33.030 align:middle line:84%
and they know that the president
is less interventionist

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.050 align:middle line:84%
and they want them to intervene
in the conflict, they don't say,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:35.810 align:middle line:90%
I'm interventionist.

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:37.970 align:middle line:84%
They say, oh, this conflict
is just really serious.

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:38.870 align:middle line:84%
It's really important
you intervene.

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:40.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm not saying it because
I'm interventionist.

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:41.470 align:middle line:84%
It's just the nature
of the conflict

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:43.090 align:middle line:84%
means we really
have to intervene.

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:45.813 align:middle line:84%
Or someone who always
wants to cut interest rates

00:23:45.813 --> 00:23:48.230 align:middle line:84%
isn't going to say, oh, let's
cut interest rates because I

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:50.270 align:middle line:90%
love cutting interest rates.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:52.215 align:middle line:84%
They're going to
say, inflation's

00:23:52.215 --> 00:23:53.090 align:middle line:90%
not really a problem.

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:54.673 align:middle line:84%
We're not really
having any inflation.

00:23:54.673 --> 00:23:56.190 align:middle line:84%
I think this is
what you should do.

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:58.670 align:middle line:84%
So the sender has an
incentive to misrepresent

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:01.890 align:middle line:84%
what the state is in order
to get their ideal decision.

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:03.910 align:middle line:84%
Because they know if
the receiver believes

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:05.670 align:middle line:84%
the state is
actually t plus beta,

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:07.890 align:middle line:84%
then the receiver will
take decision t plus beta,

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.098 align:middle line:84%
and that will give the sender
exactly what they want.

00:24:10.098 --> 00:24:12.840 align:middle line:84%
So we see that indeed, this
cannot be an equilibrium,

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:14.757 align:middle line:84%
because the sender has
a profitable deviation,

00:24:14.757 --> 00:24:16.715 align:middle line:84%
and then the receiver
won't trust the messages,

00:24:16.715 --> 00:24:17.900 align:middle line:90%
and everything breaks down.

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:19.660 align:middle line:90%
So not an equilibrium.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:25.800 align:middle line:90%


00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:27.600 align:middle line:84%
But now we have a
bit of a puzzle.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.120 align:middle line:84%
We definitely see communication
in practice between people

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:32.960 align:middle line:90%
who have misaligned interests.

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:35.420 align:middle line:84%
We don't just see this
no-communication equilibrium.

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:37.400 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
well, if we can't

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:39.667 align:middle line:84%
get a perfect
communication equilibrium,

00:24:39.667 --> 00:24:42.000 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's possible to have
an equilibrium where we have

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.420 align:middle line:90%
some partial communication.

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:44.940 align:middle line:84%
We don't perfectly
reveal the state,

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:47.420 align:middle line:84%
but we provide some valuable
information about the state.

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:49.980 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be the
key idea of this paper.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.800 align:middle line:90%


00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:56.520 align:middle line:84%
So let's our goal is to
find, well, maybe not

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.680 align:middle line:84%
perfectly informative
equilibria, but partially

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:01.100 align:middle line:90%
informative equilibrium.

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:12.040 align:middle line:90%


00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.538 align:middle line:84%
Let's start with a
really simple case.

00:25:14.538 --> 00:25:16.080 align:middle line:84%
I guess the simplest
possible message

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.360 align:middle line:84%
you could send about
some real valued state

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:20.360 align:middle line:84%
is you could say the
state's either high or low.

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:22.361 align:middle line:84%
You could just say it's
either above a threshold

00:25:22.361 --> 00:25:23.380 align:middle line:90%
or below a threshold.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:28.200 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we
can work with that.

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:32.160 align:middle line:84%
So the state is here
between 0 and 1.

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:34.280 align:middle line:84%
And let's say we
choose some threshold.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:36.520 align:middle line:90%
We'll call this threshold t1.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:39.520 align:middle line:84%
And what's the
sender going to do?

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:41.447 align:middle line:84%
If they see the
state is below t1,

00:25:41.447 --> 00:25:42.780 align:middle line:90%
they're going to send a message.

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:44.640 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we'll call the
message L. They're

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:45.980 align:middle line:90%
going to say the state is low.

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:48.280 align:middle line:90%
The state is below T1.

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:50.520 align:middle line:84%
And if they see the
state is above T1,

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:55.640 align:middle line:84%
they're going to send a message
H saying the state is high.

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:59.515 align:middle line:84%
And we want to see if this
could be an equilibrium.

00:25:59.515 --> 00:26:01.640 align:middle line:84%
You might worry because
you might think, well, wait

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:02.600 align:middle line:90%
a second.

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:05.960 align:middle line:84%
Isn't the sender going to
want to lie and pretend

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.520 align:middle line:84%
the state is high
when sometimes it's

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:10.372 align:middle line:84%
not in order to get their
higher preferred decision?

00:26:10.372 --> 00:26:12.080 align:middle line:84%
That's a good intuition,
but it turns out

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:14.788 align:middle line:84%
we can get this to work, which I
think is maybe a bit surprising.

00:26:14.788 --> 00:26:16.360 align:middle line:90%
So let's go through this.

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:20.880 align:middle line:84%
So what is y of L going to
be and y of H going to be?

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.723 align:middle line:84%
So t1 Is a parameter that
we're going to solve for.

00:26:23.723 --> 00:26:25.640 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to look
for an equilibrium that

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:27.628 align:middle line:84%
has this form for
some parameter t1.

00:26:27.628 --> 00:26:29.420 align:middle line:84%
And this is what we're
going to figure out.

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:34.060 align:middle line:84%
So what happens if the
receiver sees the message L?

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:36.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, the receiver knows
that the state is somewhere

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.480 align:middle line:90%
between 0 and T1.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:41.920 align:middle line:84%
And they know it's uniformly
distributed between 0 and T1.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:43.853 align:middle line:84%
So what do they think
is the average expected?

00:26:43.853 --> 00:26:45.520 align:middle line:84%
What do they think
is the expected state

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:46.580 align:middle line:90%
when they see message L?

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:47.487 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:26:47.487 --> 00:26:48.320 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1/2 of t1.

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:49.883 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: 1/2 of t1.

00:26:49.883 --> 00:26:51.800 align:middle line:84%
Because they're going
to say, look, all you've

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.400 align:middle line:90%
told me is the state's here.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:54.740 align:middle line:90%
It's uniformly distributed.

00:26:54.740 --> 00:26:57.580 align:middle line:84%
So on average, it's the
midpoint of this interval,

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.640 align:middle line:90%
which is t1 over 2.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:02.640 align:middle line:84%
Conversely, if the receiver
gets the message high,

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:05.530 align:middle line:84%
they're going to say, OK,
the state is uniformly

00:27:05.530 --> 00:27:09.410 align:middle line:90%
distributed between t1 and 1.

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:11.370 align:middle line:84%
So my updated
expectation of the state

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:13.410 align:middle line:84%
is just the midpoint
of this interval, which

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:15.370 align:middle line:90%
is the average of t1 and 1.

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:19.790 align:middle line:84%
So this is just going
to be T1 plus 1 over 2.

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:24.290 align:middle line:90%


00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:26.750 align:middle line:90%
Now, we're not done yet.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:32.672 align:middle line:90%


00:27:32.672 --> 00:27:34.130 align:middle line:84%
We still have to
worry what happens

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:38.533 align:middle line:84%
if they get a message that's
neither L nor H, right?

00:27:38.533 --> 00:27:40.450 align:middle line:84%
But there's kind of a
trick that we can always

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:43.288 align:middle line:84%
use that goes back to
what we did over here.

00:27:43.288 --> 00:27:45.330 align:middle line:84%
The trick was, if we see
a message that we're not

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.150 align:middle line:84%
supposed to see, we
can just treat it

00:27:47.150 --> 00:27:49.150 align:middle line:84%
as if it were a message
we were supposed to see.

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:51.330 align:middle line:84%
And that's never going to
be a profitable deviation.

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:53.750 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, the
receiver can say, look,

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:55.890 align:middle line:84%
if you send me any
message other than L or H,

00:27:55.890 --> 00:28:00.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to treat that
as if you had sent me L. So

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:03.570 align:middle line:90%
it's going to be t1 over 2.

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:08.508 align:middle line:84%
So as if m equals
L. And then there's

00:28:08.508 --> 00:28:11.050 align:middle line:84%
never a reason for the sender
to deviate to any message other

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:13.463 align:middle line:84%
than L or H, because if they
send a message other than L

00:28:13.463 --> 00:28:15.630 align:middle line:84%
or H, it's just as if they're
sending the message L.

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:17.922 align:middle line:84%
So they might as well send
the message L to begin with.

00:28:17.922 --> 00:28:21.690 align:middle line:84%
And this is a
common kind of trick

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:24.770 align:middle line:90%
that's used in cheap talk games.

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:27.165 align:middle line:84%
I want to emphasize, I don't
want to create confusion

00:28:27.165 --> 00:28:27.790 align:middle line:90%
with signaling.

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:30.370 align:middle line:84%
So I want to say
this is a trick that

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:31.710 align:middle line:90%
doesn't work with signaling.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:39.868 align:middle line:90%


00:28:39.868 --> 00:28:41.410 align:middle line:84%
Let's just understand
this, because I

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:42.650 align:middle line:84%
don't want to create
confusion going back

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:43.810 align:middle line:90%
to the signaling game.

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:45.670 align:middle line:84%
What if, in the
employment context,

00:28:45.670 --> 00:28:47.450 align:middle line:84%
the employer said,
OK, you're supposed

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:48.975 align:middle line:90%
to get this level of education?

00:28:48.975 --> 00:28:50.850 align:middle line:84%
If you don't go to
school, I'll just treat it

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:51.892 align:middle line:90%
as if you went to school.

00:28:51.892 --> 00:28:54.343 align:middle line:84%
And that shouldn't be
a profitable deviation.

00:28:54.343 --> 00:28:56.010 align:middle line:84%
That's not going to
work, because they'd

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.470 align:middle line:84%
much rather not go to school
and form the same beliefs.

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:00.050 align:middle line:84%
So crucial reason
this trick works

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:04.090 align:middle line:84%
is if I can send a
message L and message m

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:06.930 align:middle line:84%
and get the same decision, I'm
indifferent between those two

00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:07.810 align:middle line:90%
things.

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:09.938 align:middle line:84%
But if the message
itself were costly,

00:29:09.938 --> 00:29:11.730 align:middle line:84%
then I wouldn't
necessarily be indifferent,

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:14.188 align:middle line:84%
because I'd have to take into
account not only the decision

00:29:14.188 --> 00:29:16.830 align:middle line:84%
it induced, but the cost
of the message I'm sending.

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:19.050 align:middle line:84%
So this is a crucial
difference between a model

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:22.035 align:middle line:84%
with payoff-irrelevant
messages and a model where

00:29:22.035 --> 00:29:23.910 align:middle line:84%
the actions I'm choosing
are payoff-relevant.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:25.285 align:middle line:84%
I that's a bit of
a subtle point.

00:29:25.285 --> 00:29:27.570 align:middle line:84%
So any questions
on that difference?

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:28.530 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:31.017 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could you
repeat the example?

00:29:31.017 --> 00:29:32.350 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: The employee example?

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:32.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:34.690 align:middle line:84%
So in the employee
example, let's say we're

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:37.050 align:middle line:90%
doing the job market signaling.

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:39.530 align:middle line:84%
And everyone is supposed
to get-- let's look

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:43.010 align:middle line:84%
at the pooling equilibrium,
where everyone goes to college

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:44.410 align:middle line:90%
and gets a certain wage.

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:45.890 align:middle line:84%
And the employer
says, OK, if you

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:47.790 align:middle line:84%
choose any other
level of education,

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:51.310 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to treat it as
if you'd gone to college.

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:54.370 align:middle line:84%
And I'll pay you the same wage
as if you'd gone to college.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:57.870 align:middle line:84%
Well now, the student is
going to say, wait a second.

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:01.100 align:middle line:84%
If I can instead of going
to college not go to college

00:30:01.100 --> 00:30:03.753 align:middle line:84%
and get the same wage,
I'm strictly better off.

00:30:03.753 --> 00:30:06.420 align:middle line:84%
And the difference here is, even
though those two actions induce

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:08.640 align:middle line:84%
the same decision
by the receiver,

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:10.540 align:middle line:84%
the actions themselves
have different costs.

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:12.957 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the student
would rather not go to college.

00:30:12.957 --> 00:30:16.420 align:middle line:84%
Here, the message
L and the message m

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:17.633 align:middle line:90%
induce the same decision.

00:30:17.633 --> 00:30:19.300 align:middle line:84%
But in this case, the
sender is actually

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:20.800 align:middle line:84%
indifferent between
those two things

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:24.120 align:middle line:84%
because the message itself
doesn't directly have a cost.

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:25.380 align:middle line:90%
Does that clear things up?

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:26.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, great.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:27.840 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:30.600 align:middle line:90%


00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:34.500 align:middle line:84%
And actually, that's why
what I said earlier--

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:36.380 align:middle line:84%
off-path beliefs and
belief consistency

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:38.672 align:middle line:84%
don't really make a big
difference in cheap talk games.

00:30:38.672 --> 00:30:40.100 align:middle line:84%
And everything we
do is basically

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:41.767 align:middle line:84%
the same as just doing
Nash equilibrium.

00:30:41.767 --> 00:30:43.160 align:middle line:90%
But that's a subtle point.

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:44.160 align:middle line:90%
That's not so important.

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:45.575 align:middle line:90%
So here we are.

00:30:45.575 --> 00:30:47.200 align:middle line:84%
Let's see if this is
going to work out.

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:49.780 align:middle line:84%
We've specified the
receiver strategy.

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:52.580 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to
check that the sender is

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:56.100 align:middle line:84%
willing to send these messages
as they're supposed to.

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.900 align:middle line:84%
So what's the sender
supposed to do?

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:02.660 align:middle line:84%
If they see any state
here, they send message L.

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:05.940 align:middle line:84%
And if they see any state
here, they send message H.

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:07.740 align:middle line:84%
So how can that be
optimal for them?

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:11.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, what must be true if
the state is exactly t1?

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:12.320 align:middle line:90%
So let's ask this.

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:18.580 align:middle line:90%


00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:20.660 align:middle line:84%
What must be true
about the sender

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:21.880 align:middle line:90%
if the state is exactly t1?

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:22.540 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:24.665 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The sender is
indifferent between sending

00:31:24.665 --> 00:31:25.360 align:middle line:90%
L and sending H.

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:26.220 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: They have
to be indifferent.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:28.938 align:middle line:84%
Because if the state were
just a little bit to the left,

00:31:28.938 --> 00:31:31.480 align:middle line:84%
they'd want to send L. And if
it's a little bit to the right,

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:32.820 align:middle line:90%
they'd want to send m.

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.220 align:middle line:84%
So the only way they can
have those preferences

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:38.700 align:middle line:84%
is if at t1, they're exactly
indifferent between sending

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:42.720 align:middle line:84%
the message L and the message
H. So the idea is here,

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:44.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be
exactly indifferent.

00:31:44.380 --> 00:31:46.200 align:middle line:84%
And if the state is
anywhere to the left,

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:49.500 align:middle line:84%
I want to send L and anywhere
to the right I want to send H.

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:53.000 align:middle line:84%
So what we need is we
must have indifference.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:57.180 align:middle line:90%


00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:01.020 align:middle line:90%
So let's work this out.

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:04.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, that means when
the state is t1--

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:09.300 align:middle line:90%


00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:10.800 align:middle line:84%
maybe we'll write
it graphically.

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:26.308 align:middle line:90%
What we must have is y of L.

00:32:26.308 --> 00:32:27.600 align:middle line:90%
Maybe we'll put negatives here.

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:28.940 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't matter.

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:33.500 align:middle line:84%
So if I know the state is
t1, if I send the message L,

00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:36.380 align:middle line:84%
the receiver is going
to take decision y of L.

00:32:36.380 --> 00:32:38.580 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to be my utility.

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:40.860 align:middle line:84%
If I send the message
H, the receiver

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:42.940 align:middle line:84%
is going to take the
decision yH and this

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:44.340 align:middle line:90%
is going to be my utility.

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:46.590 align:middle line:84%
So in order for me to be
indifferent between these two

00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:49.270 align:middle line:84%
things, I need these
two formulas to agree.

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:51.020 align:middle line:84%
But let's look at it
graphically because I

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:53.590 align:middle line:90%
think that's a bit clearer.

00:32:53.590 --> 00:32:59.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, graphically, we have
a quadratic loss function.

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:01.430 align:middle line:90%
I'm the sender.

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:05.415 align:middle line:90%
I know the state is t1.

00:33:05.415 --> 00:33:06.790 align:middle line:84%
What I'm going to
graph is, well,

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:09.350 align:middle line:84%
what does my utility
look like as a function

00:33:09.350 --> 00:33:11.310 align:middle line:90%
of the decision y that's taken?

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:13.570 align:middle line:84%
Well, my utility is going
to look something like this.

00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:16.190 align:middle line:90%


00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:20.590 align:middle line:84%
Here it's actually
going to be 0.

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:24.710 align:middle line:84%
So if the decision y is
exactly t1 plus beta,

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:26.890 align:middle line:84%
well, that's the best
thing that I can get.

00:33:26.890 --> 00:33:29.710 align:middle line:84%
The decision exactly
matches t1 plus beta.

00:33:29.710 --> 00:33:33.070 align:middle line:84%
The difference between the
decision and t1 plus beta is 0.

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:34.230 align:middle line:90%
My loss is 0.

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:36.910 align:middle line:84%
And that's the best
possible thing I could get.

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:39.490 align:middle line:84%
If the decision is
higher than t1 plus beta,

00:33:39.490 --> 00:33:42.190 align:middle line:84%
well, I'm going to experience
a loss that's quadratic.

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:43.990 align:middle line:84%
And if it's lower
than t1 plus beta,

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:47.350 align:middle line:84%
I'm also going to experience
a loss that's quadratic.

00:33:47.350 --> 00:33:51.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, the nice thing about the
quadratic is it's symmetric.

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:52.890 align:middle line:90%
So what we need--

00:33:52.890 --> 00:34:02.550 align:middle line:90%


00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:04.870 align:middle line:84%
well, we need the picture
to look something like this.

00:34:04.870 --> 00:34:07.710 align:middle line:84%
We need the sender to be
indifferent between the decision

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:09.870 align:middle line:90%
yL and the decision yH.

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.630 align:middle line:84%
And what that means is
the utility function

00:34:12.630 --> 00:34:16.550 align:middle line:84%
has to take the same value
at these two decisions.

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:18.909 align:middle line:84%
It crosses through the
same horizontal line.

00:34:18.909 --> 00:34:21.270 align:middle line:84%
But because of
symmetry, this is only

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:24.989 align:middle line:84%
going to happen if t1 plus
beta is exactly halfway

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:28.909 align:middle line:90%
between these two points.

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:31.469 align:middle line:84%
If t1 plus beta
was closer to yL,

00:34:31.469 --> 00:34:34.949 align:middle line:84%
then the sender would rather
induce the decision yL.

00:34:34.949 --> 00:34:37.230 align:middle line:84%
And if t1 plus beta
was closer to yH,

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:39.350 align:middle line:84%
they'd rather induce
the decision yH.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:40.858 align:middle line:90%
So we could do it algebraically.

00:34:40.858 --> 00:34:42.650 align:middle line:84%
But I think it's clearer
to do it this way.

00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:49.550 align:middle line:84%
So what we see is t1
plus beta must be halfway

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:51.110 align:middle line:90%
between these two things.

00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:58.590 align:middle line:84%
So it must be yL
plus y of H over 2.

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:00.570 align:middle line:84%
Now let's do just a
little bit of algebra.

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:03.390 align:middle line:90%


00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:06.290 align:middle line:84%
Maybe let's multiply both sides
by 2 to make it a little easier.

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:12.350 align:middle line:84%
We have 2t1 plus 2 beta is
equal to y of L plus y of H.

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:13.810 align:middle line:90%
And let's just plug these in.

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:17.790 align:middle line:90%
So y of L is t1 over 2.

00:35:17.790 --> 00:35:22.410 align:middle line:90%
y of H is t1 plus 1 over 2.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:25.750 align:middle line:90%


00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:30.390 align:middle line:90%
And that's t1 plus 1/2.

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:34.230 align:middle line:84%
I just put the t1 over 2
and t1 over two together.

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:39.870 align:middle line:84%
And now let's do a
final simplification.

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:49.330 align:middle line:90%
Get t1 equals 1/2 minus 2 beta.

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:53.910 align:middle line:90%


00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:58.370 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we can
visualize what this looks like.

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:01.870 align:middle line:90%


00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:04.050 align:middle line:84%
If beta is 0, let's
start with that case.

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:05.390 align:middle line:90%
That's the easiest case.

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:10.430 align:middle line:84%
If beta is 0, then
t1 equals 1/2.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:12.430 align:middle line:84%
So that means that
the sender just says,

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:16.390 align:middle line:84%
is the state above
average or below average?

00:36:16.390 --> 00:36:22.810 align:middle line:84%
But if beta gets larger,
then t1 has to be below 1/2.

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:25.510 align:middle line:90%


00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:29.130 align:middle line:84%
So this interval is
smaller than this interval.

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:35.750 align:middle line:90%


00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:40.790 align:middle line:84%
Do you have some intuition
for why these intervals are

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:44.230 align:middle line:90%
kind of asymmetric in this way?

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:47.520 align:middle line:84%
Why is it that the
interval on the right

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:51.540 align:middle line:84%
is larger than the
interval on the left?

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:56.240 align:middle line:90%


00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:57.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:37:02.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's going to be
certain kind of values that are

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:07.420 align:middle line:84%
right below 1/2 where with
the bias in mind, our sender,

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:11.640 align:middle line:84%
they'd be more incentivized
if the responder played high

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:13.300 align:middle line:90%
or believed that it was high.

00:37:13.300 --> 00:37:16.020 align:middle line:84%
And so for that kind of
small range of values,

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:18.818 align:middle line:84%
that's why t1 is
biased below 1/2.

00:37:18.818 --> 00:37:19.860 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly right.

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:20.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:24.200 align:middle line:84%
So if it was symmetric,
then at the midpoint,

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:26.660 align:middle line:84%
the sender would always want
to say the state is high.

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:28.160 align:middle line:84%
So we have to create
these intervals

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:32.040 align:middle line:84%
to be asymmetric to ensure
that the sender isn't tempted

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:32.742 align:middle line:90%
to say high.

00:37:32.742 --> 00:37:34.200 align:middle line:84%
And effectively
what we're doing is

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:40.040 align:middle line:84%
we're shifting the midpoint left
in order to counter that bias.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:43.080 align:middle line:84%
So one way I like to
maybe think about it

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:46.160 align:middle line:84%
is, this interval
is smaller, which

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:48.680 align:middle line:84%
means the sender is giving
more precise information

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:50.080 align:middle line:90%
about the state.

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:52.758 align:middle line:84%
So in order to deter the sender
from claiming the state is high,

00:37:52.758 --> 00:37:54.800 align:middle line:84%
what we're saying is,
well, if you say it's high,

00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:56.820 align:middle line:84%
you're not able to convey
very much information,

00:37:56.820 --> 00:37:59.237 align:middle line:84%
and therefore the decision
isn't going to be very precise.

00:37:59.237 --> 00:38:01.200 align:middle line:84%
So the sender is
trading off the bias

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:04.140 align:middle line:84%
of the decision against the
informativeness of the decision.

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:06.200 align:middle line:84%
That's one way of
thinking about it.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:07.460 align:middle line:90%
And then one final thing.

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:08.560 align:middle line:90%
Wait a second.

00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:11.200 align:middle line:90%
t1 needs to be between 0 and 1.

00:38:11.200 --> 00:38:12.840 align:middle line:84%
So we immediately
see that beta has

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:15.708 align:middle line:84%
to be pretty small in order
for this to make sense at all.

00:38:15.708 --> 00:38:16.500 align:middle line:90%
Is that a question?

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:17.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:18.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you
explain the whole thing

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:20.000 align:middle line:84%
about biasness
about the decision,

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:25.800 align:middle line:84%
and what benefit there is from
the decision in the lower part

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:30.015 align:middle line:90%
versus being biased in favor?

00:38:30.015 --> 00:38:31.140 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: I guess that's--

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:33.800 align:middle line:90%


00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:36.840 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think that
maybe another way

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:44.680 align:middle line:84%
of saying it is by having
this interval larger,

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:47.200 align:middle line:84%
we're basically shifting the
midpoint of this interval

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:48.720 align:middle line:90%
farther away from me.

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:52.820 align:middle line:84%
So as the person right
at the threshold,

00:38:52.820 --> 00:38:56.240 align:middle line:84%
I'm tempted to claim the state
as high just because I'm biased

00:38:56.240 --> 00:38:58.640 align:middle line:84%
and I want the
state to be higher.

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:01.620 align:middle line:84%
But as we enlarge
this right interval,

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:03.935 align:middle line:84%
well, the decision that
is induced when I say high

00:39:03.935 --> 00:39:06.560 align:middle line:84%
starts getting higher and higher
because the interval is so big

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:08.540 align:middle line:84%
and the midpoint is moving
farther to the right.

00:39:08.540 --> 00:39:10.040 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, in
the extreme case,

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:13.840 align:middle line:84%
the interval is going to be so
big that I actually don't want

00:39:13.840 --> 00:39:15.250 align:middle line:90%
to send the higher message.

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:17.500 align:middle line:84%
And the interval is going
to be just the right length.

00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:19.180 align:middle line:84%
That kind of balances
those forces.

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:21.280 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I guess the
point that I'm making

00:39:21.280 --> 00:39:24.120 align:middle line:84%
is the length of the
interval determines

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:25.680 align:middle line:84%
how far the midpoint
of the interval

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:27.460 align:middle line:84%
is from the endpoint
of the interval.

00:39:27.460 --> 00:39:28.620 align:middle line:84%
And if we made that
interval really,

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:30.520 align:middle line:84%
really big, then the
midpoint of that interval

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:32.820 align:middle line:84%
is going to be really, really
far away from its left point.

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:34.612 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, it's going
to be too high even

00:39:34.612 --> 00:39:36.200 align:middle line:90%
for the biased sender.

00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:38.339 align:middle line:84%
So we have to balance
these just right.

00:39:38.339 --> 00:39:38.881 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:39:38.881 --> 00:39:41.530 align:middle line:90%


00:39:41.530 --> 00:39:43.530 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So I
interpret the midpoint

00:39:43.530 --> 00:39:46.190 align:middle line:84%
being far away from
the point as saying,

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:48.648 align:middle line:84%
because I'm not revealing much
information about the state,

00:39:48.648 --> 00:39:50.523 align:middle line:84%
the receiver's updated
belief about the state

00:39:50.523 --> 00:39:51.870 align:middle line:90%
is very far from the true state.

00:39:51.870 --> 00:39:55.690 align:middle line:84%
But maybe that's
not so important.

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:58.490 align:middle line:84%
So one observation
here is that--

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:09.170 align:middle line:84%
note that this only works
if 2 beta is less than 1/2,

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:11.270 align:middle line:84%
which means beta
is less than 1/4.

00:40:11.270 --> 00:40:13.883 align:middle line:90%


00:40:13.883 --> 00:40:15.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:16.327 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, absolutely.

00:40:16.327 --> 00:40:17.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, questions are great.

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:17.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:17.910 --> 00:40:20.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If t1
moves to the left,

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:23.410 align:middle line:84%
then the midpoint of
the right interval

00:40:23.410 --> 00:40:25.542 align:middle line:84%
goes down because
it's 1 plus t1.

00:40:25.542 --> 00:40:26.250 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Right.

00:40:26.250 --> 00:40:28.970 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm thinking about
is the relative position

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:30.930 align:middle line:90%
of the midpoint relative to t1.

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:33.930 align:middle line:84%
So what happens is if I move
t1 to the left, t1 moves

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:36.250 align:middle line:90%
to the left by, say, an inch.

00:40:36.250 --> 00:40:39.330 align:middle line:84%
The midpoint moves to the
left by only half an inch.

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:43.970 align:middle line:84%
So the difference between the
endpoint and the midpoint grows.

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:46.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And then
at a certain point,

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:50.890 align:middle line:84%
because of the way the
receiver is going to respond,

00:40:50.890 --> 00:40:53.618 align:middle line:84%
it's a smaller difference
from the left side?

00:40:53.618 --> 00:40:54.410 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:55.590 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
what we've done here.

00:40:55.590 --> 00:40:58.010 align:middle line:84%
We found-- I mean, this
formula is exactly telling us

00:40:58.010 --> 00:40:59.458 align:middle line:90%
where that point has to be.

00:40:59.458 --> 00:41:01.750 align:middle line:84%
So you're right, maybe I
should have been more precise.

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:06.310 align:middle line:84%
It's true as I move t1 left that
the high decision gets smaller.

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:08.130 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't get
smaller fast enough,

00:41:08.130 --> 00:41:12.110 align:middle line:84%
because when you move
the endpoint by 1,

00:41:12.110 --> 00:41:14.465 align:middle line:84%
you only move the
average by 1/2.

00:41:14.465 --> 00:41:16.590 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly-- I
mean, you can see that here.

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:23.210 align:middle line:84%
So I guess what I'm saying is
y of H is t1 plus 1 over 2.

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:24.650 align:middle line:90%
If we shrink t1--

00:41:24.650 --> 00:41:29.650 align:middle line:84%
if we reduce t1 by a little
bit, then we reduce y of H

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:30.870 align:middle line:90%
by only half of that.

00:41:30.870 --> 00:41:34.250 align:middle line:84%
But t1 gets reduced
by the full amount.

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:36.558 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And then so is the
idea that the left side is

00:41:36.558 --> 00:41:38.850 align:middle line:84%
smaller than the right side
also just based on the idea

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:40.610 align:middle line:90%
that there's a beta?

00:41:40.610 --> 00:41:41.730 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly, yeah.

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:43.770 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I guess very
simply, all it's

00:41:43.770 --> 00:41:47.810 align:middle line:84%
saying is we're
choosing the intervals

00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:50.853 align:middle line:84%
so that the midpoint of the
left interval and the midpoint

00:41:50.853 --> 00:41:53.270 align:middle line:84%
of the right interval-- if we
look at those two midpoints,

00:41:53.270 --> 00:41:57.570 align:middle line:84%
they average to the
cutoff point plus beta.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:42:01.430 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
what we wrote here.

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:04.330 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this is
the key condition.

00:42:04.330 --> 00:42:06.250 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:42:06.250 --> 00:42:09.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is the responder
aware of the bias?

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:13.490 align:middle line:84%
And if so, are they incentivized
change their behavior,

00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:16.050 align:middle line:84%
given that the sender is going
to be lying part of the time

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:16.990 align:middle line:90%
when they say high?

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:17.670 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK, great question.

00:42:17.670 --> 00:42:19.462 align:middle line:84%
So first, this goes to
an earlier question.

00:42:19.462 --> 00:42:21.770 align:middle line:90%
They do know the bias exactly.

00:42:21.770 --> 00:42:23.610 align:middle line:90%
And this is an equilibrium.

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:27.565 align:middle line:84%
So they recognize what
the sender is doing.

00:42:27.565 --> 00:42:29.190 align:middle line:84%
But I wouldn't say
the sender is lying.

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:30.648 align:middle line:84%
I mean, what the
sender is doing is

00:42:30.648 --> 00:42:33.140 align:middle line:84%
the sender is telling them
whether or not the state is

00:42:33.140 --> 00:42:35.300 align:middle line:90%
below t1 or above t1.

00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:38.780 align:middle line:84%
And given that message, the
receiver is behaving optimally.

00:42:38.780 --> 00:42:41.860 align:middle line:84%
Because if they
get the message L,

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:43.580 align:middle line:84%
whatever the bias
of the sender, they

00:42:43.580 --> 00:42:47.460 align:middle line:84%
believe that the
sender's message means

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:50.080 align:middle line:84%
the state is uniform
over this interval.

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:52.205 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, their optimal
decision is t1 over 2.

00:42:52.205 --> 00:42:54.497 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, you're thinking
exactly about the right way.

00:42:54.497 --> 00:42:56.500 align:middle line:84%
This is what the
equilibrium is capturing.

00:42:56.500 --> 00:42:58.840 align:middle line:84%
But the receiver doesn't have
any incentive to deviate.

00:42:58.840 --> 00:42:59.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:59.340 --> 00:42:59.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That makes sense.

00:42:59.960 --> 00:43:00.220 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:01.240 align:middle line:90%
Yes, other questions?

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:04.272 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So does
the receiver know t1?

00:43:04.272 --> 00:43:05.480 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh, good question.

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:06.300 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:09.560 align:middle line:84%
So t1 is a parameter
of the equilibrium.

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:10.880 align:middle line:90%
So t1 is going to be known.

00:43:10.880 --> 00:43:12.180 align:middle line:90%
And the reason is we--

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:14.480 align:middle line:84%
or this gets into a subtle
question of what's known.

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:19.460 align:middle line:84%
But in equilibrium, when
the equilibrium is played,

00:43:19.460 --> 00:43:21.460 align:middle line:84%
the players have correct
beliefs about what

00:43:21.460 --> 00:43:22.740 align:middle line:90%
the other players are doing.

00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:25.420 align:middle line:84%
So the receiver
believes that the sender

00:43:25.420 --> 00:43:27.380 align:middle line:84%
is following this
equilibrium strategy

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:29.120 align:middle line:90%
for this particular value of t1.

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:31.980 align:middle line:84%
So the receiver, in
a sense, knows t1.

00:43:31.980 --> 00:43:33.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:35.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:35.700 --> 00:43:37.358 align:middle line:84%
So I guess we have
to be careful.

00:43:37.358 --> 00:43:38.900 align:middle line:84%
I was writing down
all these formulas

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:40.840 align:middle line:84%
to try to derive
the value of t1.

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:43.620 align:middle line:84%
But once I've solved for t1,
formally, the equilibrium

00:43:43.620 --> 00:43:46.055 align:middle line:90%
is this exact value of t1.

00:43:46.055 --> 00:43:47.680 align:middle line:84%
It's not an abstract
parameter anymore.

00:43:47.680 --> 00:43:49.000 align:middle line:90%
It has to be 1/2 minus 2 beta.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:50.708 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, the
equilibrium wouldn't work.

00:43:50.708 --> 00:43:51.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:52.260 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:55.240 align:middle line:84%
So we said this only works
if beta is less than 1/4.

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:58.420 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, you can show that
if beta is more than 1/4,

00:43:58.420 --> 00:44:02.860 align:middle line:84%
the only equilibrium is the bad
no-communication equilibrium

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:03.900 align:middle line:90%
over here.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:06.620 align:middle line:84%
So let's make this
point over here.

00:44:06.620 --> 00:44:14.140 align:middle line:84%
If beta is greater
than or equal to 1/4,

00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:22.680 align:middle line:84%
no communication is
the only equilibrium.

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:28.780 align:middle line:90%


00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:32.007 align:middle line:84%
Now, when I say only, I mean,
the message m* that we use could

00:44:32.007 --> 00:44:34.340 align:middle line:84%
be a different message, but
we're never going to be able

00:44:34.340 --> 00:44:35.480 align:middle line:90%
to convey information.

00:44:35.480 --> 00:44:37.020 align:middle line:84%
And the point here
is the bias is

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:40.972 align:middle line:84%
so strong that it's impossible
to credibly convey information.

00:44:40.972 --> 00:44:43.180 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of this
in the extreme case as kind

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:46.120 align:middle line:84%
of like the story of the worker
who goes to the firm and says,

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:47.500 align:middle line:90%
I'm a good worker.

00:44:47.500 --> 00:44:50.140 align:middle line:84%
If the advisor has such a
bias relative to the receiver

00:44:50.140 --> 00:44:51.973 align:middle line:84%
and they always want
higher decisions,

00:44:51.973 --> 00:44:54.140 align:middle line:84%
the receiver just can't
listen to anything they say.

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:55.940 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
only equilibrium

00:44:55.940 --> 00:44:58.300 align:middle line:90%
is going to be no communication.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:02.660 align:middle line:84%
If beta is less than 1/4,
well, we can have some coarse

00:45:02.660 --> 00:45:06.020 align:middle line:84%
communication where we reveal
what the state is above or below

00:45:06.020 --> 00:45:07.540 align:middle line:90%
this threshold.

00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:10.860 align:middle line:84%
But we might ask, well, can we
convey even more information?

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:13.802 align:middle line:84%
Could we have more precise
information conveyed?

00:45:13.802 --> 00:45:16.260 align:middle line:84%
Do you have ideas how we might
get beyond this case of just

00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:19.860 align:middle line:84%
above or below a threshold
and actually convey--

00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:24.600 align:middle line:84%
how might we convey even more
information in equilibrium?

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:25.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:25.100 --> 00:45:25.695 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:25.695 --> 00:45:26.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I had a question.

00:45:26.820 --> 00:45:29.830 align:middle line:84%
So I was just wondering, for the
utility functions, because we

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:33.650 align:middle line:84%
say that t is between 0 and 1
and then we also have this bias,

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:37.302 align:middle line:84%
is the sender always
incentivized to--

00:45:37.302 --> 00:45:41.490 align:middle line:84%
like if t plus beta is
ever greater than 1,

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:45.923 align:middle line:84%
wouldn't that mean their
utility is always negative?

00:45:45.923 --> 00:45:46.590 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:45:46.590 --> 00:45:48.610 align:middle line:84%
So one thing is I
wouldn't-- yeah,

00:45:48.610 --> 00:45:51.750 align:middle line:90%
this is maybe a good point.

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:54.350 align:middle line:84%
You might worry-- the way
we've written the game,

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:56.867 align:middle line:84%
the utilities are
actually always negative.

00:45:56.867 --> 00:45:59.450 align:middle line:84%
Remember, with utilities, we can
always add a constant to them

00:45:59.450 --> 00:46:00.950 align:middle line:84%
and it doesn't
really change things.

00:46:00.950 --> 00:46:02.910 align:middle line:84%
So you might be thinking,
oh they'd rather not

00:46:02.910 --> 00:46:03.785 align:middle line:90%
play the game at all.

00:46:03.785 --> 00:46:05.950 align:middle line:84%
They'd rather just
not participate.

00:46:05.950 --> 00:46:09.070 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine
the utility being 100

00:46:09.070 --> 00:46:11.570 align:middle line:90%
minus this and 100 minus this.

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:14.470 align:middle line:84%
And then the utilities
would always be positive.

00:46:14.470 --> 00:46:18.083 align:middle line:84%
So you can always--
the sender is only

00:46:18.083 --> 00:46:19.750 align:middle line:84%
looking at differences
in utilities, not

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:20.765 align:middle line:90%
the absolute levels.

00:46:20.765 --> 00:46:22.390 align:middle line:84%
So we could always
shift up the utility

00:46:22.390 --> 00:46:23.810 align:middle line:84%
and it's not going to
make any difference.

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:24.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:26.550 align:middle line:84%
So if you wanted, we
could call this 100

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:31.870 align:middle line:84%
minus this and 100 minus this,
and it wouldn't change anything.

00:46:31.870 --> 00:46:33.590 align:middle line:90%
Hopefully that answers that.

00:46:33.590 --> 00:46:35.050 align:middle line:90%
So any ideas?

00:46:35.050 --> 00:46:37.065 align:middle line:84%
We have this
equilibrium where I just

00:46:37.065 --> 00:46:39.690 align:middle line:84%
say the state is either below a
threshold or above a threshold.

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:41.510 align:middle line:84%
How could we maybe convey
more precise information

00:46:41.510 --> 00:46:42.177 align:middle line:90%
about the state?

00:46:42.177 --> 00:46:42.677 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:42.677 --> 00:46:44.385 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Still along
the threshold idea,

00:46:44.385 --> 00:46:46.350 align:middle line:84%
but is it possible to
have low, medium, high?

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:47.330 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I think
that's a natural thing.

00:46:47.330 --> 00:46:48.650 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you just say
it's low, medium, high?

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:51.230 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe we could go a step
further and split it up and say

00:46:51.230 --> 00:46:53.470 align:middle line:84%
it's either really low or a
little bit low or a little bit

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:54.303 align:middle line:90%
high or really high.

00:46:54.303 --> 00:46:55.810 align:middle line:84%
And it's not clear
where this stops.

00:46:55.810 --> 00:46:56.650 align:middle line:90%
We could do a lot of this.

00:46:56.650 --> 00:46:57.950 align:middle line:90%
So that's going to be the idea.

00:46:57.950 --> 00:47:01.030 align:middle line:84%
And these are called
partition equilibria.

00:47:01.030 --> 00:47:02.890 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll erase this.

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:17.167 align:middle line:90%


00:47:17.167 --> 00:47:18.750 align:middle line:84%
So more generally,
we're going to look

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:25.130 align:middle line:84%
at what are called
partitional equilibria.

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:29.830 align:middle line:90%


00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:32.470 align:middle line:84%
This was a special case of
a partitional equilibrium

00:47:32.470 --> 00:47:34.145 align:middle line:90%
where we just had two cells.

00:47:34.145 --> 00:47:36.270 align:middle line:84%
We had two messages that
said either below or above

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:37.170 align:middle line:90%
a threshold.

00:47:37.170 --> 00:47:41.670 align:middle line:84%
But more generally,
here's our interval 0, 1.

00:47:41.670 --> 00:47:43.170 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we do
something like this?

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:47.150 align:middle line:90%
We could say t1, t2.

00:47:47.150 --> 00:47:48.830 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it keeps going.

00:47:48.830 --> 00:47:51.410 align:middle line:90%
tk minus 1.

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:54.950 align:middle line:90%


00:47:54.950 --> 00:47:58.190 align:middle line:84%
And we could say either the
state is in this interval

00:47:58.190 --> 00:48:01.310 align:middle line:84%
or it's in this interval
or it's in this interval.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:04.330 align:middle line:84%
It keeps going-- or this
interval or this interval.

00:48:04.330 --> 00:48:06.830 align:middle line:84%
So basically our
message by the sender

00:48:06.830 --> 00:48:10.110 align:middle line:84%
is going to reveal which of
these intervals the state is in.

00:48:10.110 --> 00:48:12.670 align:middle line:84%
And as the number of
intervals gets really big

00:48:12.670 --> 00:48:14.510 align:middle line:84%
and each of the intervals
gets really small,

00:48:14.510 --> 00:48:16.593 align:middle line:84%
we're actually converging
to more and more precise

00:48:16.593 --> 00:48:18.230 align:middle line:90%
communication.

00:48:18.230 --> 00:48:22.150 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand
what's going on here.

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:26.910 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of convenient to
call this t0 and call this tk.

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:30.590 align:middle line:84%
So formally, I'm going to look
at a partitional equilibrium

00:48:30.590 --> 00:48:31.570 align:middle line:90%
with k cells.

00:48:31.570 --> 00:48:34.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is a k-cell
partitional equilibrium

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:37.450 align:middle line:84%
where k is greater
than or equal to 2.

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:41.990 align:middle line:90%


00:48:41.990 --> 00:48:45.110 align:middle line:84%
So we have the first cell, the
second cell, the third cell,

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:47.190 align:middle line:90%
all the way up to the k-th cell.

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:49.150 align:middle line:84%
And that means we're
going to have k messages.

00:48:49.150 --> 00:48:50.050 align:middle line:90%
And let's label them.

00:48:50.050 --> 00:48:53.710 align:middle line:84%
Let's just call this
message 1, message 2,

00:48:53.710 --> 00:48:55.335 align:middle line:90%
all the way to message k.

00:48:55.335 --> 00:48:56.710 align:middle line:84%
So what the sender
is going to do

00:48:56.710 --> 00:48:59.335 align:middle line:84%
is they're going to look at the
state and they're going to say,

00:48:59.335 --> 00:49:00.990 align:middle line:84%
well, if the state
is between 0 and t1,

00:49:00.990 --> 00:49:02.870 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to send message m1.

00:49:02.870 --> 00:49:06.390 align:middle line:84%
If it's between t1 and t2
I'm going to send message m2.

00:49:06.390 --> 00:49:07.950 align:middle line:84%
If it's between t2
and t3, I'm going

00:49:07.950 --> 00:49:10.250 align:middle line:84%
to send message m3,
all the way up to mk.

00:49:10.250 --> 00:49:15.150 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:17.790 align:middle line:90%
And maybe I'll call this y1.

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:19.800 align:middle line:84%
If the receiver sees
message m1, they're

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:21.460 align:middle line:90%
going to take decision y1.

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:26.720 align:middle line:84%
If they see m2, they're
going to take y2, and so on.

00:49:26.720 --> 00:49:31.520 align:middle line:84%
So formally, what I mean is
yk is equal to y of m of k.

00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:35.560 align:middle line:84%
But this is just
cleaner notation.

00:49:35.560 --> 00:49:39.400 align:middle line:84%
And again, we're going to try to
solve for these parameter values

00:49:39.400 --> 00:49:42.560 align:middle line:84%
t1 up to tk minus 1, we're going
to solve for these thresholds

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:45.523 align:middle line:84%
to see if we can make
this an equilibrium.

00:49:45.523 --> 00:49:46.940 align:middle line:84%
So what's going
to have to happen?

00:49:46.940 --> 00:49:49.660 align:middle line:84%
Well first, let's use
receiver optimality.

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:54.800 align:middle line:90%


00:49:54.800 --> 00:49:57.560 align:middle line:84%
Receiver optimality says,
well, when the receiver gets

00:49:57.560 --> 00:50:00.480 align:middle line:84%
this message m1, they know
the state is somewhere

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:02.360 align:middle line:90%
between 0 and t1.

00:50:02.360 --> 00:50:04.520 align:middle line:84%
And they believe, in fact,
the state is uniformly

00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:06.320 align:middle line:90%
distributed between 0 and t1.

00:50:06.320 --> 00:50:09.160 align:middle line:84%
So their updated belief
about the expected state

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:11.200 align:middle line:84%
is that it's the
midpoint of this.

00:50:11.200 --> 00:50:13.800 align:middle line:84%
So in each case, the
receiver's decision

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:17.200 align:middle line:84%
must be the midpoint of
the cell that they're

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:18.260 align:middle line:90%
told the state is in.

00:50:18.260 --> 00:50:22.700 align:middle line:84%
So in math language, what
we would say is yk or yj--

00:50:22.700 --> 00:50:25.680 align:middle line:90%


00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:27.760 align:middle line:84%
let's make sure we
get this right-- is

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:37.720 align:middle line:84%
going to be equal to tj
minus 1 plus tj over 2.

00:50:37.720 --> 00:50:40.360 align:middle line:84%
Because when they
get message mj,

00:50:40.360 --> 00:50:46.400 align:middle line:84%
they learn that the state is
between tj minus 1 and tj.

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:49.040 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we'll say
this here, because they

00:50:49.040 --> 00:50:58.780 align:middle line:84%
learn that the state t is
between tj minus 1 t and tj.

00:50:58.780 --> 00:51:02.480 align:middle line:90%


00:51:02.480 --> 00:51:04.280 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:08.280 align:middle line:84%
So that gives us a formula
for each of these decisions.

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:13.500 align:middle line:84%
This is for j equals
1 all the way up to k.

00:51:13.500 --> 00:51:16.720 align:middle line:90%


00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:25.320 align:middle line:84%
And then what does sender
optimality tell us?

00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:27.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's going to
be the same idea.

00:51:27.200 --> 00:51:30.080 align:middle line:84%
At each of these
split points, we

00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:33.080 align:middle line:84%
want the sender to be
indifferent between sending

00:51:33.080 --> 00:51:35.120 align:middle line:84%
the lower message and
the higher message.

00:51:35.120 --> 00:51:37.040 align:middle line:84%
So when the state
is t1, the sender

00:51:37.040 --> 00:51:39.400 align:middle line:84%
should be indifferent
between m1 and m2.

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:41.720 align:middle line:84%
When the state is t2,
the sender should be

00:51:41.720 --> 00:51:43.480 align:middle line:90%
indifferent between m2 and m3.

00:51:43.480 --> 00:51:45.720 align:middle line:84%
All the way up when the
state is tk minus 1,

00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:48.840 align:middle line:84%
they should be indifferent
between mk minus 1 and mk.

00:51:48.840 --> 00:51:55.800 align:middle line:84%
So what that means is
that tj plus beta should

00:51:55.800 --> 00:52:08.380 align:middle line:84%
be the midpoint of yj
and yj plus 1 over 2.

00:52:08.380 --> 00:52:12.160 align:middle line:84%
Let's make sure we got kind of
the fence post problem right.

00:52:12.160 --> 00:52:13.810 align:middle line:84%
When the state is
t1, we should be

00:52:13.810 --> 00:52:18.670 align:middle line:84%
indifferent between m1 and
m2, which is yj and yj plus 1.

00:52:18.670 --> 00:52:21.650 align:middle line:90%
So that makes sense.

00:52:21.650 --> 00:52:24.130 align:middle line:90%
And if we did a little more--

00:52:24.130 --> 00:52:32.850 align:middle line:84%
and this must be true for
j equals 1 to k minus 1.

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:37.170 align:middle line:84%
Notice we have k
conditions for the receiver

00:52:37.170 --> 00:52:39.170 align:middle line:90%
because we have k cells.

00:52:39.170 --> 00:52:41.730 align:middle line:84%
We have k minus 1
conditions for the sender

00:52:41.730 --> 00:52:44.275 align:middle line:84%
because we have k
minus 1 split points.

00:52:44.275 --> 00:52:46.150 align:middle line:84%
This is the classic kind
of fence post issue,

00:52:46.150 --> 00:52:52.570 align:middle line:84%
that k minus 1 cuts
gives us k pieces.

00:52:52.570 --> 00:52:54.290 align:middle line:90%
So we could do a bit of algebra.

00:52:54.290 --> 00:52:57.670 align:middle line:84%
I don't think we really need
to go through all the algebra.

00:52:57.670 --> 00:53:00.990 align:middle line:84%
Let me just kind of describe
what you're going to find.

00:53:00.990 --> 00:53:05.410 align:middle line:90%


00:53:05.410 --> 00:53:06.970 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll just
say mathematically

00:53:06.970 --> 00:53:10.730 align:middle line:90%
what this is called is--

00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:25.810 align:middle line:84%
OK, we get kind of a
system of equations for t1

00:53:25.810 --> 00:53:29.410 align:middle line:90%
through tk minus 1.

00:53:29.410 --> 00:53:31.930 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we already did
the case of k equals 2.

00:53:31.930 --> 00:53:35.170 align:middle line:84%
And in the case of k equals
2, we just solve for t1.

00:53:35.170 --> 00:53:39.390 align:middle line:84%
But more generally, we have
to solve for k minus 1 values.

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:44.930 align:middle line:90%


00:53:44.930 --> 00:53:50.310 align:middle line:84%
But I want to just maybe
convey the idea intuitively.

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:53.690 align:middle line:90%


00:53:53.690 --> 00:53:55.190 align:middle line:84%
Here's how I like
to think about it.

00:53:55.190 --> 00:53:57.890 align:middle line:84%
We could do the algebra, but
let's just think about it.

00:53:57.890 --> 00:54:00.650 align:middle line:84%
Let's look at
adjacent cells, OK?

00:54:00.650 --> 00:54:03.250 align:middle line:84%
And let's look at what happens
when the state is here.

00:54:03.250 --> 00:54:04.330 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:54:04.330 --> 00:54:08.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, if these adjacent
cells had the same length,

00:54:08.930 --> 00:54:10.410 align:middle line:90%
would this--

00:54:10.410 --> 00:54:12.530 align:middle line:90%
let's call this tj.

00:54:12.530 --> 00:54:14.770 align:middle line:84%
Would the sender be
indifferent between getting

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:15.910 align:middle line:90%
this point and this point?

00:54:15.910 --> 00:54:16.910 align:middle line:90%
Which would they prefer?

00:54:16.910 --> 00:54:22.530 align:middle line:90%


00:54:22.530 --> 00:54:27.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, these two decisions
are equidistant from tj.

00:54:27.370 --> 00:54:28.830 align:middle line:90%
But the sender is biased.

00:54:28.830 --> 00:54:33.710 align:middle line:84%
The sender prefers the decision
that's closest to tj plus beta.

00:54:33.710 --> 00:54:37.730 align:middle line:84%
So the sender would always
prefer the higher decision.

00:54:37.730 --> 00:54:42.410 align:middle line:84%
So what we need is
we need tj plus beta

00:54:42.410 --> 00:54:47.730 align:middle line:84%
to be equal to the average
of these two points.

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:51.570 align:middle line:84%
So what we need to do is we need
the average of these two points

00:54:51.570 --> 00:55:03.302 align:middle line:90%
to shift right by beta.

00:55:03.302 --> 00:55:06.010 align:middle line:84%
Because if we want the sender to
be indifferent between these two

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:08.060 align:middle line:84%
points, they shouldn't
average to tj.

00:55:08.060 --> 00:55:12.500 align:middle line:84%
They should average
to tj plus beta.

00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:15.920 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to do is we
want to move out this interval,

00:55:15.920 --> 00:55:18.940 align:middle line:84%
make this interval longer, until
the average has shifted right

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:20.340 align:middle line:90%
by beta.

00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:24.900 align:middle line:84%
But we're only
moving this point.

00:55:24.900 --> 00:55:27.380 align:middle line:90%
This point is staying fixed.

00:55:27.380 --> 00:55:29.420 align:middle line:84%
So if we want the
average to go up by beta,

00:55:29.420 --> 00:55:31.800 align:middle line:84%
we have to increase
this point by 2 beta.

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:39.590 align:middle line:90%


00:55:39.590 --> 00:55:41.885 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why are we
keeping the left point?

00:55:41.885 --> 00:55:42.760 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Say again.

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:45.427 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why is the
left point fixed?

00:55:45.427 --> 00:55:47.760 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: We're basically
solving the equation forward.

00:55:47.760 --> 00:55:51.280 align:middle line:84%
We're saying, let's suppose
the left interval is like this.

00:55:51.280 --> 00:55:52.840 align:middle line:84%
How big should the
next interval be?

00:55:52.840 --> 00:55:55.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: OK,
because [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:55.820 --> 00:55:59.860 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So we have to
increase this point by 2 beta

00:55:59.860 --> 00:56:01.940 align:middle line:90%
to increase the average by beta.

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:04.340 align:middle line:84%
But how can we increase the
midpoint of this interval

00:56:04.340 --> 00:56:04.977 align:middle line:90%
by 2 beta?

00:56:04.977 --> 00:56:07.060 align:middle line:84%
How much do we have to
increase the right endpoint

00:56:07.060 --> 00:56:10.900 align:middle line:84%
to get the midpoint
to go up by 2 beta?

00:56:10.900 --> 00:56:11.560 align:middle line:90%
4 beta.

00:56:11.560 --> 00:56:16.620 align:middle line:84%
So that means this has
to go up by 4 beta.

00:56:16.620 --> 00:56:18.620 align:middle line:84%
We could do the algebra,
but I just kind of like

00:56:18.620 --> 00:56:19.500 align:middle line:90%
to think it through.

00:56:19.500 --> 00:56:24.027 align:middle line:84%
So what we indeed have is
the general formula is this.

00:56:24.027 --> 00:56:25.860 align:middle line:84%
And you can solve it,
but I think it's nicer

00:56:25.860 --> 00:56:28.740 align:middle line:90%
to think of it like this.

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:29.800 align:middle line:90%
Let's write it this way.

00:56:29.800 --> 00:56:40.620 align:middle line:90%


00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:42.460 align:middle line:90%
So we're comparing the lengths--

00:56:42.460 --> 00:56:45.540 align:middle line:90%
ah, no, no, that's wrong.

00:56:45.540 --> 00:56:46.840 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, let me rewrite this.

00:56:46.840 --> 00:56:59.100 align:middle line:90%


00:56:59.100 --> 00:57:03.020 align:middle line:84%
So we get this formula,
which compares the lengths

00:57:03.020 --> 00:57:05.180 align:middle line:90%
of the consecutive intervals.

00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:12.120 align:middle line:84%
So in our picture here,
this is an interval length.

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:16.780 align:middle line:90%


00:57:16.780 --> 00:57:18.760 align:middle line:84%
This is the next
interval length.

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:23.060 align:middle line:90%


00:57:23.060 --> 00:57:25.280 align:middle line:84%
And what we're saying is,
as we move to the right,

00:57:25.280 --> 00:57:26.900 align:middle line:84%
the next interval
moving to the right

00:57:26.900 --> 00:57:30.288 align:middle line:84%
must be as long as the interval
to the left plus 2 beta

00:57:30.288 --> 00:57:32.080 align:middle line:84%
through the argument
that we did over here.

00:57:32.080 --> 00:57:33.033 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:57:33.033 --> 00:57:34.700 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you
originally first drew

00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:38.900 align:middle line:84%
the picture, that was saying
like tj plus 1 minus tj was

00:57:38.900 --> 00:57:41.440 align:middle line:84%
the same length as tj minus
[INAUDIBLE] tj minus 1

00:57:41.440 --> 00:57:42.220 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:57:42.220 --> 00:57:43.532 align:middle line:90%
And then basically [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:43.532 --> 00:57:44.240 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Right.

00:57:44.240 --> 00:57:47.500 align:middle line:84%
So formally in this
picture, I fixed tj minus 1.

00:57:47.500 --> 00:57:48.820 align:middle line:90%
I fixed tj.

00:57:48.820 --> 00:57:51.793 align:middle line:84%
And I was saying, how
big must tj plus 1 be?

00:57:51.793 --> 00:57:53.960 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it would have been
easier just to the algebra.

00:57:53.960 --> 00:57:55.280 align:middle line:90%
But I find this clearer.

00:57:55.280 --> 00:57:56.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:57:56.740 --> 00:57:57.860 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:57.860 --> 00:57:59.820 align:middle line:90%
And actually, we can check.

00:57:59.820 --> 00:58:02.470 align:middle line:84%
Let's go back to our
example over here.

00:58:02.470 --> 00:58:05.210 align:middle line:84%
When t1 was equal
to 1/2 minus 2 beta,

00:58:05.210 --> 00:58:08.070 align:middle line:84%
what were the lengths
of these intervals?

00:58:08.070 --> 00:58:13.790 align:middle line:84%
This interval was
1/2 minus 2 beta.

00:58:13.790 --> 00:58:15.090 align:middle line:90%
What was the other interval?

00:58:15.090 --> 00:58:18.070 align:middle line:90%


00:58:18.070 --> 00:58:21.030 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's 1 minus that, right?

00:58:21.030 --> 00:58:30.870 align:middle line:84%
1 minus 1/2 minus 2 beta,
which equals 1/2 plus 2 beta.

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:33.630 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, that formula
held in this special case.

00:58:33.630 --> 00:58:35.990 align:middle line:84%
What that midpoint was
doing is it was exactly

00:58:35.990 --> 00:58:38.270 align:middle line:84%
ensuring that the
length of this interval

00:58:38.270 --> 00:58:40.930 align:middle line:84%
was exactly the length of
this interval plus 4 beta.

00:58:40.930 --> 00:58:43.790 align:middle line:90%


00:58:43.790 --> 00:58:45.790 align:middle line:84%
And where did beta
over 4 come in?

00:58:45.790 --> 00:58:48.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, 4 beta has
to be less than 1.

00:58:48.610 --> 00:58:50.290 align:middle line:90%
And that's where it comes from.

00:58:50.290 --> 00:58:53.030 align:middle line:90%


00:58:53.030 --> 00:58:57.390 align:middle line:84%
So now we can see what the
structure of the equilibria

00:58:57.390 --> 00:59:02.590 align:middle line:84%
have to look like if we you
want to draw it more carefully.

00:59:02.590 --> 00:59:04.270 align:middle line:84%
As we move to the
right, the intervals

00:59:04.270 --> 00:59:06.650 align:middle line:84%
are going to keep getting
bigger by a fixed amount.

00:59:06.650 --> 00:59:10.950 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be
something like this.

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:13.190 align:middle line:84%
The first interval
has some length delta.

00:59:13.190 --> 00:59:15.990 align:middle line:84%
The next interval has some
length delta plus 4 beta.

00:59:15.990 --> 00:59:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And the next interval has
some length delta plus 8 beta.

00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:20.590 align:middle line:90%
And we can keep going.

00:59:20.590 --> 00:59:24.430 align:middle line:84%
And then we have to make sure
that everything works out

00:59:24.430 --> 00:59:26.830 align:middle line:84%
at the right interval and
everything comes together.

00:59:26.830 --> 00:59:29.270 align:middle line:90%
So what's the takeaway?

00:59:29.270 --> 00:59:32.390 align:middle line:90%
What we actually find--

00:59:32.390 --> 00:59:34.450 align:middle line:84%
let me just give the
statement over here.

00:59:34.450 --> 00:59:45.750 align:middle line:90%


00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:49.310 align:middle line:84%
So it can be shown-- we
won't go through it--

00:59:49.310 --> 01:00:04.510 align:middle line:84%
that there exists a partitional
equilibrium with k cells

01:00:04.510 --> 01:00:19.370 align:middle line:84%
if and only if
beta is less than--

01:00:19.370 --> 01:00:26.412 align:middle line:90%


01:00:26.412 --> 01:00:27.870 align:middle line:84%
and I think this
formula can really

01:00:27.870 --> 01:00:29.970 align:middle line:84%
capture a lot of the key
insights of the paper.

01:00:29.970 --> 01:00:32.463 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to understand
what's going on.

01:00:32.463 --> 01:00:34.630 align:middle line:84%
First, let's check that
this is consistent with what

01:00:34.630 --> 01:00:35.830 align:middle line:90%
we did before.

01:00:35.830 --> 01:00:38.310 align:middle line:90%
So what if we put in k equals 2?

01:00:38.310 --> 01:00:43.030 align:middle line:84%
With k equals 2, we're back to
the case, we already looked at.

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:46.190 align:middle line:84%
And with k equals 2, the theorem
says there exists a partitional

01:00:46.190 --> 01:00:50.510 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium with two cells if
and only if beta is less than 1

01:00:50.510 --> 01:00:54.590 align:middle line:84%
over 2 times 2 times
1, which is 1/4.

01:00:54.590 --> 01:00:57.640 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, it agrees with
what we got over here.

01:00:57.640 --> 01:01:00.520 align:middle line:84%
More generally, we get
a formula like this.

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:01.580 align:middle line:90%
So what does it say?

01:01:01.580 --> 01:01:05.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, as k increases,
communication

01:01:05.640 --> 01:01:07.560 align:middle line:90%
becomes more precise.

01:01:07.560 --> 01:01:10.560 align:middle line:84%
As k increases, we're
partitioning the state

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:14.120 align:middle line:84%
into smaller and smaller
cells, and therefore conveying

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:16.260 align:middle line:84%
more and more precise
information about the state.

01:01:16.260 --> 01:01:27.560 align:middle line:90%


01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:29.900 align:middle line:84%
So if we want more
precise communication,

01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:32.760 align:middle line:84%
what it's telling us is
beta has to be even smaller.

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:37.220 align:middle line:90%
So that requires less bias.

01:01:37.220 --> 01:01:43.880 align:middle line:90%


01:01:43.880 --> 01:01:50.600 align:middle line:84%
So what we see is that the
less bias that the agent is,

01:01:50.600 --> 01:01:52.920 align:middle line:84%
the more precisely it's
possible for the sender

01:01:52.920 --> 01:01:55.480 align:middle line:84%
to communicate with
that agent and more

01:01:55.480 --> 01:01:58.280 align:middle line:84%
we can split the state space
into these very, very tiny cells

01:01:58.280 --> 01:02:01.120 align:middle line:84%
and convey more and more
precise information.

01:02:01.120 --> 01:02:03.100 align:middle line:84%
And in the limiting case,
when there's no bias,

01:02:03.100 --> 01:02:04.933 align:middle line:84%
we can basically perfectly
reveal the state.

01:02:04.933 --> 01:02:05.768 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:02:05.768 --> 01:02:07.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you
explain a little bit more

01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:10.200 align:middle line:84%
about the reasoning between
precise communication

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:14.132 align:middle line:84%
coming from less bias
or the last arrow?

01:02:14.132 --> 01:02:14.840 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Right.

01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:17.440 align:middle line:84%
So I'm saying the last arrow
comes from this equation.

01:02:17.440 --> 01:02:23.040 align:middle line:84%
If k grows-- so we want to have
an equilibrium with more cells--

01:02:23.040 --> 01:02:25.760 align:middle line:84%
then this inequality
has to be satisfied.

01:02:25.760 --> 01:02:29.600 align:middle line:84%
Because k is on the bottom of
this fraction, as k goes up,

01:02:29.600 --> 01:02:31.120 align:middle line:90%
this fraction gets smaller.

01:02:31.120 --> 01:02:32.717 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, beta
has to be smaller

01:02:32.717 --> 01:02:34.300 align:middle line:84%
in order for this
equilibrium to work.

01:02:34.300 --> 01:02:35.717 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
a reason that's

01:02:35.717 --> 01:02:37.440 align:middle line:90%
not related to this inequality?

01:02:37.440 --> 01:02:38.980 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh, yeah.

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:41.560 align:middle line:84%
I think the reason not
related to the inequality

01:02:41.560 --> 01:02:46.560 align:middle line:84%
is that when we provide really,
really precise information

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:50.680 align:middle line:84%
about the state, it becomes
very tempting for the sender

01:02:50.680 --> 01:02:54.320 align:middle line:84%
to misreport and claim the
state is higher than it is.

01:02:54.320 --> 01:02:57.640 align:middle line:84%
So think of the logic
of that opening case

01:02:57.640 --> 01:02:59.020 align:middle line:90%
where we fully reveal the state.

01:02:59.020 --> 01:03:00.770 align:middle line:84%
If we basically say
what the state is down

01:03:00.770 --> 01:03:02.540 align:middle line:84%
to really, really
strong precision,

01:03:02.540 --> 01:03:03.420 align:middle line:90%
what's the sender going to do?

01:03:03.420 --> 01:03:04.940 align:middle line:84%
They're just going to
claim the state is higher,

01:03:04.940 --> 01:03:06.815 align:middle line:84%
they're going to claim
it's in a higher cell,

01:03:06.815 --> 01:03:09.840 align:middle line:84%
and they're going to
benefit from that.

01:03:09.840 --> 01:03:12.160 align:middle line:84%
If they're less biased,
then it's feasible for us

01:03:12.160 --> 01:03:14.180 align:middle line:84%
to split the state space
into very fine cells,

01:03:14.180 --> 01:03:17.995 align:middle line:84%
and the sender won't have
an incentive to deviate.

01:03:17.995 --> 01:03:19.620 align:middle line:84%
So I guess one final
picture I'll show.

01:03:19.620 --> 01:03:21.537 align:middle line:84%
And I know this lecture
is maybe a bit harder,

01:03:21.537 --> 01:03:26.240 align:middle line:84%
so I think I'll end maybe
5 or 10 minutes early.

01:03:26.240 --> 01:03:29.040 align:middle line:84%
But what we can
look at is I think

01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:32.080 align:middle line:84%
it's helpful to look
at the space of biases.

01:03:32.080 --> 01:03:34.320 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at beta.

01:03:34.320 --> 01:03:38.400 align:middle line:84%
And let's see what's
possible in this game.

01:03:38.400 --> 01:03:41.400 align:middle line:90%
So here, we have 1/4.

01:03:41.400 --> 01:03:44.860 align:middle line:84%
And if beta is up here, then
no communication is possible.

01:03:44.860 --> 01:03:57.520 align:middle line:90%


01:03:57.520 --> 01:03:59.148 align:middle line:84%
The agent is so
biased that there's

01:03:59.148 --> 01:04:01.440 align:middle line:84%
no equilibrium in which any
information about the state

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:02.220 align:middle line:90%
is revealed.

01:04:02.220 --> 01:04:05.000 align:middle line:84%
That's what we already
said over there.

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:06.660 align:middle line:90%
Let's get the numbers right.

01:04:06.660 --> 01:04:11.546 align:middle line:84%
If we have 3, we have
6, it's already 1/12.

01:04:11.546 --> 01:04:16.240 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I think we'll get
down to something like 1/12.

01:04:16.240 --> 01:04:27.360 align:middle line:84%
So here, there is a
two-cell equilibrium only.

01:04:27.360 --> 01:04:29.880 align:middle line:84%
If the bias is
between 1/12 and 1/4,

01:04:29.880 --> 01:04:33.182 align:middle line:84%
then the only equilibrium
that can be shown is the kind

01:04:33.182 --> 01:04:35.640 align:middle line:84%
of equilibrium we described,
where you either say the state

01:04:35.640 --> 01:04:37.140 align:middle line:90%
is above or below a threshold.

01:04:37.140 --> 01:04:39.792 align:middle line:90%


01:04:39.792 --> 01:04:42.000 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to keep
going down and down and down.

01:04:42.000 --> 01:04:44.360 align:middle line:84%
And then here in
this case, we're

01:04:44.360 --> 01:04:49.810 align:middle line:84%
going to have a two-cell and
a three-cell equilibrium.

01:04:49.810 --> 01:04:51.690 align:middle line:84%
So for a lower
level of bias, there

01:04:51.690 --> 01:04:53.970 align:middle line:84%
will be a two-cell equilibrium,
where the sender just

01:04:53.970 --> 01:04:56.290 align:middle line:84%
says the state's above
or below a threshold.

01:04:56.290 --> 01:04:58.770 align:middle line:84%
There will also be a
three-cell equilibria,

01:04:58.770 --> 01:05:02.370 align:middle line:84%
where the sender says the state
is either low, medium, or high.

01:05:02.370 --> 01:05:02.990 align:middle line:90%
But that's it.

01:05:02.990 --> 01:05:04.290 align:middle line:90%
There's nothing else.

01:05:04.290 --> 01:05:06.130 align:middle line:84%
And then if we get
down to here, there's

01:05:06.130 --> 01:05:09.290 align:middle line:84%
going to be a two-cell, a
three-cell, and a four-cell.

01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:13.210 align:middle line:84%
And as we keep
going, as beta keeps

01:05:13.210 --> 01:05:16.070 align:middle line:84%
getting smaller and smaller,
the set of equilibria grows.

01:05:16.070 --> 01:05:17.510 align:middle line:84%
We have more and
more equilibria.

01:05:17.510 --> 01:05:19.468 align:middle line:84%
And we have equilibria
with more and more cells

01:05:19.468 --> 01:05:21.570 align:middle line:84%
that provide more and more
precise communication.

01:05:21.570 --> 01:05:23.990 align:middle line:84%
And the limit as beta
gets really close to 0,

01:05:23.990 --> 01:05:26.070 align:middle line:84%
we have very
informative equilibria.

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:43.810 align:middle line:90%


01:05:43.810 --> 01:05:48.050 align:middle line:84%
And maybe one final
observation I'll make

01:05:48.050 --> 01:05:52.570 align:middle line:84%
is let's suppose beta is
small enough so that there's

01:05:52.570 --> 01:05:53.810 align:middle line:90%
a range of equilibria.

01:05:53.810 --> 01:05:56.630 align:middle line:84%
There's a one-cell, there's a
two-cell, there's a three-cell,

01:05:56.630 --> 01:05:57.570 align:middle line:90%
there's a four-cell.

01:05:57.570 --> 01:06:00.410 align:middle line:84%
The question is,
which equilibrium

01:06:00.410 --> 01:06:01.670 align:middle line:90%
is best for the players?

01:06:01.670 --> 01:06:10.705 align:middle line:90%


01:06:10.705 --> 01:06:11.830 align:middle line:90%
And the intuition for this.

01:06:11.830 --> 01:06:14.117 align:middle line:84%
Let's say we're in a case--
let's say beta is here.

01:06:14.117 --> 01:06:15.950 align:middle line:84%
So we have both a
two-cell and a three-cell.

01:06:15.950 --> 01:06:16.750 align:middle line:90%
What do you think is better?

01:06:16.750 --> 01:06:17.347 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:06:17.347 --> 01:06:18.430 align:middle line:90%
Or do you have a question?

01:06:18.430 --> 01:06:18.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:06:18.610 --> 01:06:20.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say,
I think for the receiver,

01:06:20.050 --> 01:06:21.630 align:middle line:84%
it's probably better
to have more cells.

01:06:21.630 --> 01:06:22.790 align:middle line:90%
So they're getting closer.

01:06:22.790 --> 01:06:23.832 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly right.

01:06:23.832 --> 01:06:25.690 align:middle line:84%
So it's definitely
better for the receiver,

01:06:25.690 --> 01:06:28.230 align:middle line:84%
because if there's more
cells in the equilibrium,

01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:29.490 align:middle line:84%
the receiver is just
getting more information

01:06:29.490 --> 01:06:30.250 align:middle line:90%
about the state.

01:06:30.250 --> 01:06:33.025 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, they're able to
make more accurate decisions.

01:06:33.025 --> 01:06:35.650 align:middle line:84%
It may be less obvious, but it's
actually better for the sender

01:06:35.650 --> 01:06:36.490 align:middle line:90%
as well.

01:06:36.490 --> 01:06:45.410 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out the
highest k is best for both

01:06:45.410 --> 01:06:48.010 align:middle line:90%
the sender and the receiver.

01:06:48.010 --> 01:06:50.640 align:middle line:84%
So for any value of beta,
depending on the value of beta,

01:06:50.640 --> 01:06:52.390 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be a
range of equilibria.

01:06:52.390 --> 01:06:54.230 align:middle line:84%
There's going to be some
uninformative equilibria.

01:06:54.230 --> 01:06:55.850 align:middle line:84%
In fact, there's always the
babbling equilibrium where

01:06:55.850 --> 01:06:57.050 align:middle line:90%
you don't reveal anything.

01:06:57.050 --> 01:06:59.350 align:middle line:84%
There might be a two-cell where
I just say it's high or low.

01:06:59.350 --> 01:07:00.808 align:middle line:84%
There might be a
three-cell where I

01:07:00.808 --> 01:07:02.230 align:middle line:90%
say it's high, medium, or low.

01:07:02.230 --> 01:07:04.930 align:middle line:84%
A four cell-- the
biggest number of cells

01:07:04.930 --> 01:07:07.063 align:middle line:84%
that can exist as
in equilibrium,

01:07:07.063 --> 01:07:09.230 align:middle line:84%
that's going to be the most
informative equilibrium.

01:07:09.230 --> 01:07:11.647 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that's going
to be the equilibrium that's

01:07:11.647 --> 01:07:15.570 align:middle line:84%
best for the sender and
also for the receiver.

01:07:15.570 --> 01:07:17.672 align:middle line:84%
So maybe that's some sort
of prediction about what

01:07:17.672 --> 01:07:18.630 align:middle line:90%
we might expect to see.

01:07:18.630 --> 01:07:19.210 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Amy.

01:07:19.210 --> 01:07:20.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So it makes sense
that it's best for the receiver

01:07:20.890 --> 01:07:22.290 align:middle line:84%
just because when
you get closer,

01:07:22.290 --> 01:07:23.210 align:middle line:90%
the receiver [INAUDIBLE].

01:07:23.210 --> 01:07:23.730 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

01:07:23.730 --> 01:07:25.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And how does
the sender benefit?

01:07:25.480 --> 01:07:28.343 align:middle line:90%
Is it also the same reason?

01:07:28.343 --> 01:07:29.010 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

01:07:29.010 --> 01:07:35.250 align:middle line:84%
It's actually a property
of the quadratic law.

01:07:35.250 --> 01:07:39.370 align:middle line:84%
So I think the intuition is that
because the receiver is always

01:07:39.370 --> 01:07:42.380 align:middle line:84%
matching the decision with
their expectation of the state,

01:07:42.380 --> 01:07:46.120 align:middle line:84%
the sender can never
shift the decision.

01:07:46.120 --> 01:07:48.160 align:middle line:84%
So we're getting into
a technical point.

01:07:48.160 --> 01:07:50.220 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out the
expected decision is always

01:07:50.220 --> 01:07:53.820 align:middle line:84%
going to be equal to
the expected state.

01:07:53.820 --> 01:07:57.140 align:middle line:84%
And as the cells get
smaller, the benefit

01:07:57.140 --> 01:07:59.200 align:middle line:84%
is that the state is
conveyed more precisely,

01:07:59.200 --> 01:08:01.100 align:middle line:84%
and therefore
higher decisions are

01:08:01.100 --> 01:08:03.200 align:middle line:84%
taken in higher states and the
lower decisions in lower states.

01:08:03.200 --> 01:08:05.075 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be
better for the sender.

01:08:05.075 --> 01:08:07.640 align:middle line:84%
But the basic
intuition is that the--

01:08:07.640 --> 01:08:10.940 align:middle line:90%


01:08:10.940 --> 01:08:13.940 align:middle line:84%
I guess the formal answer would
be it's like a bias variance

01:08:13.940 --> 01:08:15.580 align:middle line:90%
decomposition.

01:08:15.580 --> 01:08:19.300 align:middle line:84%
You can express the expected
loss as the bias of the error

01:08:19.300 --> 01:08:20.677 align:middle line:90%
and the variance of the error.

01:08:20.677 --> 01:08:22.260 align:middle line:84%
And the bias is fixed
and the variance

01:08:22.260 --> 01:08:23.279 align:middle line:90%
you're going to make smaller.

01:08:23.279 --> 01:08:25.237 align:middle line:84%
But I'm happy to talk
more offline, if you want

01:08:25.237 --> 01:08:28.220 align:middle line:90%
to go into that in more depth.

01:08:28.220 --> 01:08:30.200 align:middle line:84%
So I think that was a
bit of a harder lecture,

01:08:30.200 --> 01:08:30.840 align:middle line:90%
so let me stop there.

01:08:30.840 --> 01:08:32.080 align:middle line:84%
I know we're near the
end of the semester.

01:08:32.080 --> 01:08:33.420 align:middle line:90%
I'll give people an early stop.

01:08:33.420 --> 01:08:36.890 align:middle line:84%
And I will see everyone on
Tuesday for the last lecture.

01:08:36.890 --> 01:08:40.000 align:middle line:90%